// Generado por tools/build-help.mjs desde docs/ (en). No editar a mano: npm run help.
export default {
 "intro": "<p>A-CAD is a <b>parametric</b> 2D drawing editor for iOS, Android and the browser. You draw points, lines, circles, arcs, ellipses and splines, relate them with <b>constraints</b> (horizontal, parallel, tangent...) and <b>dimensions</b>, and a geometric solver keeps everything consistent: if you move a point or change a measurement, the rest of the drawing rearranges itself. The texts in the drawing <b>calculate</b> (variables, formulas, vectors and matrices) and can drive the dimensions, and mechanisms move by hand or with <b>graphical simulations</b> over time, with actuators and graphs. It is useful for sketches, descriptive geometry, graphic statics, mechanisms and laser-cut parts.</p>",
 "chapters": {
  "conceptos": "<details data-ch=\"conceptos\"><summary>Basic concepts</summary>\n<p>The ideas behind A-CAD. If you have never used a parametric CAD, start here: with these ideas clear, the rest of the manual explains itself.</p>\n<p>Index · <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"empezar\">1. Getting started →</a></p>\n<details><summary>Parametric drawing</summary>\n<p>In a <b>parametric</b> drawing, each element keeps its relationships with the others, not just its position.</p>\n<p><b>Ordinary drawing.</b> A line is a stroke that stays where you left it.</p>\n<p><b>Parametric drawing.</b> Each element also keeps its <b>relationships</b> with the others: this line is horizontal, that circle is tangent to it, this point lies on that arc, this distance measures 40.</p>\n<p><b>What happens when you change something.</b> When you move something or change a measurement, the program repositions everything else so those relationships still hold.</p>\n<h4>Two steps</h4>\n<p>In A-CAD you work in two steps:</p>\n<ol><li><b>Draw the approximate shape</b>, without worrying about exact measurements.</li><li><b>Add constraints and dimensions</b> until the shape is the way you want it. Exact measurements come from the dimensions, not from the hand.</li></ol>\n<p><b>The advantage</b> shows up later: to change the design it is enough to change one dimension, and everything that depends on it adjusts.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Constraints</summary>\n<p>A <b>constraint</b> is a geometric relationship that is always maintained: horizontal, vertical, parallel, perpendicular, coincident, midpoint, concentric, equal, tangent or fixed.</p>\n<p><b>How to add them.</b> Select the elements and tap the constraint's button (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\">Constraints</a>).</p>\n<p><b>Automatic ones.</b> Many are created on their own while drawing: if a line ends up horizontal or tangent because you accepted the soft lock, the constraint is added (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\">Drawing</a>).</p>\n<p><b>Numbering.</b> Each constraint carries a number per type (H1, ∥2, T1...), labeled next to its elements and with which it appears in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"element-tree\">element tree</a>; only the points that end up on an element while drawing (●) are not labeled.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Degrees of freedom</summary>\n<p><b>Degrees of freedom</b> are the independent ways in which an element can move.</p>\n<ul><li>A free point has two (it can move in x and in y).</li><li>A line has those of its two endpoints.</li><li>Each constraint and each dimension removes degrees of freedom: a horizontal line can no longer tilt; if you also fix its length with a dimension, it can only translate.</li></ul>\n<h4>Fully defined</h4>\n<p>A drawing in which nothing can move without breaking a relationship is <b>fully defined</b>. You do not need to get there: in a mechanism, precisely, you leave free the movements you care about. To see what is still free, <b>drag</b>: whatever moves still has freedom.</p>\n<h4>Typical errors</h4>\n<p>There are two:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Conflicting constraints</b>: two relationships that cannot both be satisfied, such as a line that is horizontal and vertical at the same time. The app warns you and undoes the action.</li><li><b>Redundant constraints</b>: a relationship that others already imply. It breaks nothing, but it makes every calculation slower. The <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"redundant\" title=\"Redundant\"></span> <b>Redundant</b> button finds them (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"redundant-constraints\">Redundant constraints</a>).</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>The solver</summary>\n<p>The <b>solver</b> is the engine that, after each change, looks for positions for all the points that satisfy all the constraints and dimensions at once.</p>\n<p><b>Which one it uses.</b> A-CAD uses <b>PlaneGCS</b>, the solver of FreeCAD's Sketcher (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"acerca\">About</a>).</p>\n<p><b>Why the approximate shape matters.</b> The solver starts from where things are and looks for the closest solution. That is why <b>the approximate shape matters</b>: if a tangency can occur on the inside or the outside, the solver picks the one closest to what you drew.</p>\n<p><b>If it does not converge.</b> If a constraint does not converge, move the elements a little closer to the position you want and try again.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Driving and reference dimensions</summary>\n<p>A <b>dimension</b> is a measurement in the drawing: distance, angle, radius or diameter (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\">Dimensions</a>).</p>\n<h4>Two types</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Driving</b>: it rules over the geometry. If you type 40, the drawing adjusts so that distance measures 40. It removes degrees of freedom, like a constraint.</li><li><b>Reference</b> (read-only, in italics): it only measures. It shows the current value without forcing anything, so it never conflicts. It is useful for reading results, for example how far a point of a mechanism travels.</li></ul>\n<h4>Names and formulas</h4>\n<p>Dimensions have names: D1 for distances, A1 for angles, R1 for radii, O1 for diameters.</p>\n<p><b>Formulas.</b> A dimension can depend on another through a formula: <code>D2 = D1/2</code>. A reference dimension can also feed a formula, so one measurement in the drawing can govern another.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Calculating in the drawing</summary>\n<p>Texts in the drawing can calculate (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\">Text and calculation</a>).</p>\n<p><b>Variables and results.</b> <code>k:25</code> defines a variable, <code>2*k=</code> shows a result, and dimension formulas can use those variables. There are also vectors, matrices and built-in functions.</p>\n<h4>Reading order</h4>\n<p>The page reads like a mathematical spreadsheet: <b>from top to bottom and, within the same row, from left to right</b>. A variable has the value of its last definition before the point where it is used, and it can be redefined further down.</p>\n<p><b>Use before defining.</b> If you use it before defining it, the app warns you that it is defined further down.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Graphical simulations</summary>\n<p>A mechanism is a drawing with degrees of freedom on purpose: when you drag a link, the solver repositions the others. A <b>simulation</b> does the same but over time (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\">Graphical simulations</a>).</p>\n<h4>How it works</h4>\n<ol><li>A <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"act-r\" title=\"Rotational actuator\"></span> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"act-l\" title=\"Linear actuator\"></span> <b>actuator</b> says how a part moves: a rotation (rotational) or a displacement (linear), with an expression of time <code>t</code> such as <code>90*t</code>, or with a table.</li><li>At each instant, the solver solves the drawing with the actuator in its position.</li><li><b>Graphs</b> record any measurement (a dimension, a point's coordinate) against time or against another measurement, and <b>paths</b> leave the trajectory of a point.</li></ol>\n<p><b>What else can be simulated.</b> Gears, rack and pinion, and relationships that hold only while two elements touch (stops, cams) extend what can be simulated.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Glossary</summary>\n<table class=\"fref\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actuator</td><td>Element that moves a part of the drawing over time: rotational (MR) or linear (ML).</td></tr><tr><td>Layer</td><td>Group of elements that are shown or hidden together.</td></tr><tr><td>Construction</td><td>Helper line, circle or arc: drawn in gray and not included in laser cutting.</td></tr><tr><td>Outline</td><td>Chain of lines, arcs and splines joined at their endpoints; closed if it returns to its start.</td></tr><tr><td>Driving dimension</td><td>Measurement that forces the geometry to have that value.</td></tr><tr><td>Reference dimension</td><td>Measurement that only reads the current value (in italics).</td></tr><tr><td>Degree of freedom</td><td>Independent way in which an element can move.</td></tr><tr><td>Array</td><td>Repeated copies of a selection, in a line or around a center.</td></tr><tr><td>Constraint</td><td>Geometric relationship that the solver always maintains.</td></tr><tr><td>Solver</td><td>Engine that repositions the geometry to satisfy all constraints and dimensions.</td></tr><tr><td>Spline</td><td>Smooth curve that passes through points of the drawing.</td></tr><tr><td>Linked</td><td>Copy that follows the original when it changes.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Index · <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"empezar\">1. Getting started →</a></p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "empezar": "<details data-ch=\"empezar\"><summary>Getting started</summary>\n<p>How to use the app: gestures with the pencil, finger and mouse, the parts of the screen, and how to select.</p>\n<details><summary>Apple Pencil or mouse</summary>\n<p>They draw and select.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Finger</summary>\n<p>The finger moves the canvas.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Pan</b>: one finger.</li><li><b>Zoom</b>: two fingers (pinch).</li><li><b>Drawing with the finger</b>: turn on <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"hand\" title=\"Finger draws\"></span> <b>Finger draws</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"view\">View</a> panel) and the finger draws too.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Mouse</summary>\n<ul><li><b>Zoom</b>: wheel.</li><li><b>Pan</b>: middle button, right button or Space + drag.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Menu (☰)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"menu\" title=\"Menu\"></span> <b>Menu (☰)</b> shows or hides the tools panel on the right. The first button on the bar opens the <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"list-tree\" title=\"Element tree\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"element-tree\"><b>element tree</b></a>.</p>\n<ul><li>The bottom bar explains the active tool and shows the data of the selected element.</li><li>Undo, redo, delete selection and fit view are on the right of the bar.</li><li>Selecting: tap an element (tap several to add). The panel buttons are only enabled if the selection suits them.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Finger magnifier</summary>\n<p>A circular magnifier with <b>2x zoom</b> lets you aim precisely with your finger.</p>\n<h4>How to use it</h4>\n<ol><li>Touch and <b>hold</b> (a quarter of a second, without moving). A circle appears with a <b>2x zoom</b> of what is under your finger, offset upward so your finger does not cover it. The crosshair in the center marks the exact point.</li><li>Without lifting, slide your finger to fine-tune.</li></ol>\n<p>If you move your finger before it appears, it is a normal pan.</p>\n<h4>When it works</h4>\n<ul><li>With <b>Finger draws</b> on, it works with any tool.</li><li>With <b>Finger draws</b> off, it works in <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"select-v\"><b>Select</b></a>: on release, whatever is under the crosshair gets selected.</li></ul>\n<p>With <b>Finger draws</b> and Select, holding means aiming: on release, whatever is under the crosshair gets selected, without moving anything or making a box (the box is made by dragging right away from an empty spot).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Cursor ring</summary>\n<p>The small circle that accompanies the pencil (over the screen) or the mouse also appears in the <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"select\" title=\"Select\"></span> <b>Select</b> tool, at the cursor's real position.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Help and search</summary>\n<p>The <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"help\" title=\"Help and documentation\"></span> <b>?</b> button in the bar opens this manual, with the index of chapters and topics on the left and the chapter on the right.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Search</b>: type one or more words (accents and capitals do not matter) and you get a list of results with their chapter and topic; tap one to go there.</li><li><b>Help for the active tool</b>: the <b>?</b> button in the bottom bar opens the topic of the tool you are using.</li><li><b>Help for a window</b>: the Data, GitHub files, Insert library, Graph and Actuator windows have their own <b>?</b>, which opens the topic that explains them.</li><li><b>Where is that button?</b>: tap a tool icon in the help; the help closes, the button is highlighted on the screen (if it is in a dropdown, the dropdown opens) and <b>Back to the help</b> appears.</li><li><b>Links</b>: a reference to another chapter or topic opens that place, and a strip at the top takes you back to where you were.</li></ul>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "guias": "<details data-ch=\"guias\"><summary>Task guides</summary>\n<p>Each guide solves a concrete task, step by step, with an example drawing that opens in the app. For the detail of each tool, follow the links to the chapter that explains it.</p>\n<p>When you open an example, the app <b>turns on in <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"view\">View</a></b> what that drawing needs to show (dimensions, constraints, texts or points) and tells you. If you later turn one of those options off, the dimensions, labels or texts of the guides will stop showing: turn it on again in View.</p>\n<details><summary>Draw a part that adapts to its measurements</summary>\n<p>A plate with a centered hole: when you change the length or the height, the plate adjusts and the hole stays at the center with a proportional diameter.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"pieza-parametrica\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Draw the plate with <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"rect\" title=\"Rectangle\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"rectangle-b\">Rectangle</a>: tap two opposite corners. It gets horizontal and vertical constraints.</li><li>Select a corner and tap <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"lock\" title=\"Fix / release\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"fix--release\">Fix / release</a>: this way the plate does not move when its measurements change.</li><li>Draw the hole with <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"circle\" title=\"Circle\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"circle-c\">Circle</a>, and a diagonal with <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"cline\" title=\"Construction line\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"construction-line-k\">Construction line</a> between opposite corners.</li><li>Select the center of the circle and the diagonal, and apply <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-mid\" title=\"Midpoint\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"midpoint\">Midpoint</a>: the hole ends up at the center of the plate.</li><li>With <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dim-dist\" title=\"Dimension\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimension-d\">Dimension (D)</a>, tap the two ends of one side to dimension the length, and those of another for the height. Tap the circle to dimension its diameter.</li><li>Double tap the diameter dimension and type a formula with another dimension, for example <code>D2/3</code> (the diameter is a third of the height). See <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"formulas\">Formulas</a>.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>If you do not see the dimensions (D1, D2, O1) or the constraint labels, turn on <b>Dimensions</b> and <b>Constraints</b> in <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"view\">View</a>. Double tap the length dimension or the height dimension and change its value: the plate adjusts, the hole stays at the center and its diameter is recalculated.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>What each type of dimension does: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\">Dimensions</a>.</li><li>All the geometric relations: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\">Constraints</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Calculate with texts and drive a dimension</summary>\n<p>A text can store a value and do calculations, and a dimension can read that value: you change a number and the drawing is recalculated.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"calculo-y-cotas\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>With <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"text\" title=\"Text\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\" data-topic=\"write-a-text-t\">Text (T)</a>, tap above the drawing and type <code>ancho:120</code>; Enter. What you type is an equation: <code>ancho:120</code> defines the variable.</li><li>In the entry below type <code>alto:ancho/2</code> and Enter, and then <code>area:ancho*alto=</code> and Enter: the <code>=</code> at the end shows the result (7200).</li><li>Draw a rectangle and dimension a horizontal side and a vertical one (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimension-d\">Dimension (D)</a>).</li><li>Double tap each dimension and, instead of a number, type <code>ancho</code> in the first and <code>alto</code> in the second.</li><li>To change the drawing, tap the <code>ancho:120</code> text, type another value and Enter.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>Everything that depends on <code>ancho</code> is recalculated: the height, the area and the two dimensions of the rectangle.</p>\n<h4>Important: the reading order</h4>\n<p>A variable is valid from the place on the page where it is defined, reading from top to bottom. That is why the texts that define variables go <b>above</b> what uses them. See <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\" data-topic=\"reading-order\">Reading order</a>.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>Everything you can write in a text: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\">Text and calculation</a>.</li><li>The available functions: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\">Functions and operators</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Solve equations</summary>\n<p>A system of equations is solved in a text, without leaving the drawing.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"ecuaciones\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>With <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"text\" title=\"Text\"></span> Text (T), define each unknown with an initial value: <code>x:1</code> and <code>y:1</code>.</li><li>Type <code>solve(x+y==10; x-y==2; x; y)=</code> and Enter. The equations use <code>==</code>; the loose names are the unknowns.</li><li>The text shows the solution: <code>x=6; y=4</code>.</li><li>Try a system that is not linear: <code>a:2</code>, <code>b:2</code> and <code>solve(a*b==12; a+b==7; a; b)=</code>.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>The second system has two solutions (3 and 4, or 4 and 3): the one that appears depends on the initial value you gave <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>All the forms of <code>solve</code>: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\" data-topic=\"equations\">Equations</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Draw a force polygon with vectors</summary>\n<p>Three forces placed head to tail and their resultant: when you move a point, the magnitudes update.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"poligono-de-fuerzas\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>With <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"line\" title=\"Line\"></span> Line (L), draw three chained lines (the end of one is the start of the next) and a fourth from the first point to the last.</li><li>Convert each line into a vector: select the line and <b>one of its ends</b>, which will be the <b>head</b> (the end the force points to), and choose <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"vector\" title=\"Vector\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"vector-w\">Vector (W)</a> in the Line dropdown. It is the same gesture as for the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"head-and-tail-one-gesture\">leader and the linear actuator</a>.</li><li>Each vector shows its magnitude as text at the midpoint. The fourth, from the tail of the first to the head of the last, is the resultant.</li><li>Drag a point: the magnitudes update by themselves.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>The magnitudes (D1, D2…) are named reference dimensions, so you can use them in formulas and in texts.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>Unit and scale of a vector (for example, kN): <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"vector-w\">Vector (W)</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Move a mechanism and graph its motion</summary>\n<p>A crank that turns with an actuator, and a graph of the height and the horizontal position of its end.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"manivela-y-grafica\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Draw a line from one point (O) to another (A), fix O with <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"lock\" title=\"Fix / release\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"fix--release\">Fix / release</a> and dimension the length (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimension-d\">Dimension (D)</a>).</li><li>With <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"act-r\" title=\"Rotary actuator\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\" data-topic=\"actuators\">MR actuator</a>, tap the line and its end O, which will be the center of rotation.</li><li>In its window type the displacement with respect to time: <code>90*t</code> (degrees; a quarter turn per second).</li><li>With <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"graph\" title=\"Graph\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\" data-topic=\"graphs\">Graphs</a>, tap where the corner of the graph goes and type one relation per line: <code>p2.y vs t</code> and <code>p2.x vs t</code> (<code>p2</code> is the name of A, which you see in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"element-tree\">element tree</a>).</li><li>Tap <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"play\" title=\"Simulation\"></span> Simulation and ▶ to play.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>A goes around O, and the graph draws two shifted waves: height and horizontal position against time. When you stop, the drawing goes back to how it was.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>Rolling, gears and rack: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\">Graphical simulations</a>.</li><li>A wheel rolling without slipping: <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"cicloide-rodando\">Open the cycloid example</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Prepare a part for laser cutting</summary>\n<p>The cut lines and the marking lines (a fold, for example) are separated by color and line type.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"corte-laser\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Draw the part: the outline, the holes and the fold line.</li><li>Select the cut lines and give them a style (for example, red and continuous) in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"style\">Style</a> panel.</li><li>Select the fold line and give it another style (for example, blue and dashed).</li><li>Choose <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"svg\" title=\"SVG for laser cutting\"></span> <b>SVG for laser cutting</b> in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"file-button\">File button</a>.</li><li>Each line class (type, color and width) is sent to <b>Cut</b>, <b>Mark</b> or <b>Skip</b>: assign Cut to the red ones and Mark to the blue one.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>A real-scale SVG in millimeters, with two groups, \"cut\" and \"mark\". Dimensions, texts, points and fills are not included. More detail in <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"svg-cut\">SVG CUT</a>.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Make a text console</summary>\n<p>A text that shows several calculated lines, like a game status or a data panel.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"consola-de-texto\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Define the variables you want to show, each in its own text: <code>$$n:3$$</code>.</li><li>In another text, define a vector of strings: <code>$$consola:[\"ESTADO\", \"Vidas: \"+num2str(n), \"Puntos: \"+num2str(n*100)]$$</code>. Each string will be one line (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\">Functions and operators</a>).</li><li>Create a third text that is just <code>$$consola=$$</code>.</li><li>Double tap it, turn on <i>Show only the result</i> and choose a monospaced font and the font and background colors (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\" data-topic=\"font-and-text-format\">Font and text format</a>).</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>Three lines of normal text: \"ESTADO\", \"Vidas: 3\" and \"Puntos: 300\", on the background you chose. Change <code>n</code> and the console updates by itself.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>A vector of strings that starts and ends with three backticks is drawn as a code block.</li><li>To make the console react to what you type, see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\" data-topic=\"data-between-drawings\">Data between drawings</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Remember a value between changes and play a sound</summary>\n<p>A counter that is not lost when you close the drawing, and a sound that plays when something changes.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"contador-y-sonido\">Open the example</a>. When opened it writes the <code>contador</code> data and copies the <code>ding</code> sound to this device (if you already have a different <code>ding</code>, yours stays).</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Type <code>$$c:rfile(contador; 0)$$</code>: it reads the data \"contador\", or is 0 if it does not exist yet.</li><li>Type <code>$$wfile(c+1; contador)=$$</code>: it stores that value plus 1. The result is 1 and all that matters is that it runs.</li><li>Type <code>$$c=$$</code> to see the value that was read.</li><li>Open <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"folder-open\" title=\"Data\"></span> <b>File → Data…</b>: <code>contador</code> is already there (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"device-data\">Device data</a>).</li><li>An audio file is added with <b>Import…</b> and given a name; the example already includes one called <code>ding</code>. Type <code>$$play(\"ding\")=$$</code> to play it (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\" data-topic=\"sounds\">Sounds</a>).</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p><code>c</code> goes up by one each time you confirm a change in the drawing, and the data survives even if you close the app. Dragging or simulating does not write the data: only confirmed changes do.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>The data is shared by all the drawings on the device: two drawings can share a value.</li><li>A name built from a variable, like <code>play(n)</code>, does not travel with the drawing.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Repeat a hole around a center</summary>\n<p>Six equal holes on a flange, which all change together when you modify one.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"patron-circular\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>With <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"circle\" title=\"Circle\"></span> draw the outline of the flange and fix its center O with <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"lock\" title=\"Fix / release\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"fix--release\">Fix / release</a>.</li><li>Draw a small circle (the hole) and dimension its distance to O and its radius (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimension-d\">Dimension (D)</a>).</li><li>Select the hole and choose <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"array-circ\" title=\"Circular array\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dividir\" data-topic=\"circular-array\">Circular array</a>.</li><li>Tap O as the <b>Center</b>, leave <b>Total angle</b> at 360° and type <b>Total number</b> 6.</li><li>Leave <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dividir\" data-topic=\"linked-copies\">linked copies</a> on.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>Six holes 60° apart. Change the distance dimension or the radius of one and all six follow.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>For a row instead of a circle: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dividir\" data-topic=\"linear-array\">Linear array</a>.</li><li>To remove the relation between copies: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dividir\" data-topic=\"without-linking\">Without linking</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Round the corners of a plate</summary>\n<p>A rectangular plate with its four corners rounded, whose radius is driven from a single dimension.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"placa-redondeada\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Draw the plate with <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"rect\" title=\"Rectangle\"></span> and dimension its length and height (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimension-d\">Dimension (D)</a>). Fix one corner so it does not move.</li><li>Select the four corners (their points) and choose <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"fillet\" title=\"Fillet\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"fillet-f-and-chamfer\">Fillet (F)</a>.</li><li>Type the radius, for example <code>8</code>. Each corner gets a tangent arc and its own radius dimension (R1 to R4).</li><li>To make the four equal, double tap R2, R3 and R4 and type the formula <code>R1'mm</code> (with the unit, their labels say \"R8 mm\").</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>Four arcs of radius 8. Change R1 and the four corners follow; change the length or the height and the plate stays rounded.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>If the radius does not fit in the corner, the app warns you and tells you the maximum.</li><li>To join with a line instead of an arc, use the chamfer (same button, <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"fillet-f-and-chamfer\">Fillet and Chamfer</a>).</li><li>A full circle or ellipse cannot be rounded.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Draw a smooth curve through points</summary>\n<p>A spline that passes through real points of the drawing and deforms when you drag them.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"curva-suave\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Choose <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"spline\" title=\"Spline\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"spline-s\">Spline (S)</a> and tap the points the curve must pass through.</li><li>To finish, tap the last point again (or Esc). Tapping the first one closes it.</li><li>Fix the two ends with <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"lock\" title=\"Fix / release\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"fix--release\">Fix / release</a> if you want them not to move.</li><li>Drag a middle point: the curve deforms and the rest of the drawing follows.</li><li>With the spline selected, open the Spline dropdown and choose <b>Close / open</b> (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"splines\" data-topic=\"close--open\">Close / open</a>).</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>A smooth curve that passes exactly through every point. Open, it has no curvature at the ends; closed, it is smooth all the way around, with no corner at the join.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>A parallel curve at a fixed distance (the roller of a cam): <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"splines\" data-topic=\"offset\">Offset</a>.</li><li>The path of a moving point, turned into a spline: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"splines\" data-topic=\"trace-point\">Trace point</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Dimension in inches and see the drawing smaller</summary>\n<p>Dimensions that say a unit (inches) while the drawing measures the real thing, and a dimension drawn to scale.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"cotas-en-pulgadas\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Draw the plate and fix one corner. Double tap the length dimension and type the value with its unit: <code>4'in</code> (the apostrophe joins the value and the unit). Do the same with the height: <code>2'in</code>.</li><li>Draw a long line and dimension it with <code>126'in</code>. At real size (3200.4 mm) it does not fit on a letter sheet, which leaves 165.9 mm usable with 25 mm margins.</li><li>Edit that dimension and add the scale in brackets: <code>126'in[*0.05]</code> (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"scaled-dimension\">Scaled dimension</a>).</li><li>To change only the unit a dimension is read in, use <b>Dimension unit</b> in its window (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimension-with-unit\">Dimension with unit</a>).</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>The plate says \"4 in\" and \"2 in\", and the drawing (in mm) measures 101.6 by 50.8. The long line is drawn at 1:20: it measures 160.02, fits on the sheet and its dimension still says \"126 in\", <u>underlined</u> to warn that it is not at real scale.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>A dimension without a unit enters formulas as a number; to add it to one with a unit, give it the unit in the formula (<code>D1'mm+D2</code>). See <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimensions-with-unit-in-formulas\">Dimensions with unit in formulas</a>.</li><li>In a read-only dimension, the unit only changes the label.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Draw forces to scale</summary>\n<p>Two forces in kN drawn with a representation scale that you change in a single place.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"escala-de-fuerzas\">Open the example</a>.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>In a text, define the scale as a variable: <code>$$eF:1'mm/kN$$</code> (each kN is drawn with 1 mm).</li><li>Draw a horizontal line and dimension it with the force and the scale: <code>30'kN[*eF]</code>.</li><li>Do the same with another line: <code>45'kN[*eF]</code>.</li><li>Change <code>eF</code> to <code>2'mm/kN</code>: both lines double in length.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>The dimensions say \"30 kN\" and \"45 kN\", and the lines measure 30 and 45 mm. Unlike a reduction scale, the dimension is <b>not</b> underlined: it is at the scale you declared.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li><code>eF</code> is read in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\" data-topic=\"reading-order\">reading order</a>: if you redefine it further down, the dimensions above it keep using the previous one.</li><li>Without a scale, <code>30'kN</code> would measure 30000 (in SI) and the app warns you with ⚠. See <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"scale-with-units\">Scale with units</a>.</li><li>Vectors use the same scale: <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"guias\" data-topic=\"draw-a-force-polygon-with-vectors\">Force polygon</a>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Create a function library and use it in another drawing</summary>\n<p>Functions you define once and reuse in any drawing, with a short prefix (the alias).</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b> <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"biblioteca-geometria\">the library</a> and <a href=\"#\" data-example=\"usar-biblioteca\">a drawing that uses it</a>. The second one carries an already inserted copy; it does not come from GitHub.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>In a new drawing, define your functions in texts: <code>$$AreaCirculo(Dext):pi*Dext^2/4$$</code> and <code>$$AreaAnillo(Dext; Dint):AreaCirculo(Dext)-AreaCirculo(Dint)$$</code>. You can leave a test, <code>$$ejemplo:AreaAnillo(50; 30)=$$</code>: examples do not travel with the library.</li><li>In <b>Settings → Library</b> check \"It is a library\" and fill in the card: name, <b>alias</b> (<code>GEO</code>), author and description (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\" data-topic=\"creating-a-library\">Create a library</a>).</li><li>Review what is <b>public</b>: by default, the functions and scripts. What those need travels too, as private.</li><li>Choose <b>Save to GitHub</b>: it goes in the <code>MyLibrary/</code> folder with a version that goes up by itself (v1, v2…). First you have to <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"github\">set up GitHub</a>.</li><li>In another drawing, choose <b><a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"file-button\">File</a> → Insert library…</b>, pick it and confirm the alias.</li><li>Use its names with the alias: <code>$$GEO.AreaAnillo(50; 30)=$$</code>.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>A block <code>▸ Basic geometry (GEO) · v1</code> with a copy of the definitions, which works offline. The <code>GEO.</code> prefix is drawn in gray and the calculation gives 1256.6 for the ring and 78.54 for the circle of diameter 10.</p>\n<h4>To continue</h4>\n<ul><li>If you change the library and save it again, the drawings that use it show <code>⟳ v2</code>; in the block's properties, \"Update\" brings the new version and first warns you which names change (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\" data-topic=\"updating\">Update</a>).</li><li>A library should not depend on the dimensions or measurements of its drawing: the check before saving warns you.</li><li>To type a name without remembering it, use the <code>ƒx</code> suggestions (<a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\">Text and calculation</a>).</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Save, open and share your drawings</summary>\n<p>Your drawings are saved as <code>.json</code> files, and you can take them to another device with GitHub.</p>\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol><li>Open the <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"save\" title=\"File\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"file-button\">File button</a> and choose <b>Save (.json)</b>: it asks for a name and downloads the file.</li><li>To open it, choose <b>Open…</b> and select the file.</li><li>To have it on another device and with history, set up <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"github\">GitHub</a> once (repository, branch, folder and a token) and use <b>Save to GitHub</b> and <b>Open from GitHub</b>.</li><li>The data saved by <code>wfile</code> and <code>rfile</code>, and the audio files, are in <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"device-data\">Device data</a>.</li></ol>\n<h4>What you will see</h4>\n<p>The app also remembers the last drawing in this browser, but that save is local only: save a file to have a backup.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "dibujar": "<details data-ch=\"dibujar\"><summary>Drawing</summary>\n<p>The tools that create geometry (points, lines, circles, arcs, ellipses, splines, rectangles and polygons) and the drawing aids: the soft lock and the intersection magnet.</p>\n<details><summary>Dropdown buttons</summary>\n<p>Buttons with a marked corner are <b>dropdowns</b>: they show the last tool used from the group.</p>\n<p>Tap the corner, tap the button again when it is already active, or press and hold it to see the others.</p>\n<h4>Groups mentioned in this chapter</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Line</b>: <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"vector\" title=\"Vector\"></span> Vector (W), <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"mline\" title=\"Midpoint line\"></span> Midpoint line (M) and <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"cline\" title=\"Construction line\"></span> Construction line (K).</li><li><b>Circle</b>: <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"circle3\" title=\"Circle through 3 points\"></span> Circle through 3 points and <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"ccircle\" title=\"Construction circle\"></span> construction circle.</li><li><b>Ellipse</b>: <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"earc\" title=\"Partial ellipse\"></span> Partial ellipse.</li><li><b>Arc</b>: <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"arc-3\" title=\"Arc by 3 points\"></span> Arc by 3 points (A) and <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"carcc\" title=\"Construction arcs\"></span> construction arcs.</li><li><b>Rectangle</b>: <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"rect-c\" title=\"Center rectangle\"></span> Center rectangle.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Point (P)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"point\" title=\"Point\"></span> <b>Point (P)</b>: on a line or circle, it is constrained to it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Line (L)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"line\" title=\"Line\"></span> <b>Line (L)</b>: tap the start and the end (it chains) or drag a segment.</p>\n<p>Esc or <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"select-v\">Select</a> ends it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Vector (W)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"vector\" title=\"Vector\"></span> <b>Vector (W)</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">Line dropdown</a>): drawn like a line, but it has an <b>arrowhead at the end point</b> (the tail is the start) and its <b>magnitude</b> written as text at the midpoint.</p>\n<h4>Magnitude text</h4>\n<p><b>Position.</b> The insertion point is the middle of the top side of the box, so it stays centered, and by default it is <b>aligned with the line</b> of the vector: it turns with it and reads from left to right.</p>\n<p><b>Normal text.</b> The magnitude text is a normal text: it can be moved, resized or given another font, or you can release the alignment (Text → Release).</p>\n<h4>Magnitude</h4>\n<p><b>Named dimension.</b> The magnitude is a named dimension (D1, D2...), read-only at first, so you can use it in formulas and texts.</p>\n<p><b>Changing it.</b> To change it, <b>double-tap the vector</b>. Its properties include:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Magnitude</b>: a value or a formula with other dimensions, such as <code>2*D1</code>; typing it fixes the length.</li><li><b>Fixed magnitude</b>: clear it to go back to measuring only.</li><li><b>Name</b>.</li></ul>\n<h4>Unit and scale</h4>\n<p><b>Unit and scale.</b> The vector's properties also have <b>Unit</b> and <b>Scale</b>: with Magnitude 30, Unit kN and Scale <code>1'mm/kN</code> (or a text variable, <code>eF</code>) the vector measures 30 mm and its text says \"30 kN\", and in formulas and texts the magnitude is worth 30 kN.</p>\n<p><b>Magnitude not fixed.</b> If the magnitude is not fixed, the unit and the scale say how much the vector's measured length is worth: the resultant of a force polygon is read from the drawing in kN (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimension-with-unit\">Dimension with unit</a>).</p>\n<h4>Converting between line and vector</h4>\n<p><b>Line to vector.</b> With a line selected, and one of its ends (it will be the <b>head</b>; if you choose none, the head is the end), choose Vector in the dropdown and the line becomes a vector. It is the same gesture as for the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"head-and-tail-one-gesture\">leader and the linear actuator</a>.</p>\n<p><b>Vector to line.</b> With a vector selected, choose Line and it stops being one (the arrow, the text and the dimension are removed).</p>\n<h4>Deleting, copying and exporting</h4>\n<p><b>Deleting and copying.</b> Deleting the vector also deletes its text; copies and arrays copy it with its own text and its own magnitude.</p>\n<p><b>Exporting.</b> The arrow appears in the SVG and the DXF (not in the laser-cut SVG, which treats it as a line).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Head and tail: one gesture</summary>\n<p>The <b>vector</b>, the <b>leader</b> and the <b>linear actuator</b> are defined the same way: you choose <b>a line and one of its ends</b>, and that end is the <b>head</b> (where the arrow tip goes); the other end is the tail.</p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"vector\" title=\"Vector\"></span> <b>Vector</b>: the line becomes a vector (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"vector-w\">Vector (W)</a>).</li><li><b>Leader</b>: the arrow goes at the chosen end (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"leaders\">Leaders</a>).</li><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"act-l\" title=\"Linear actuator\"></span> <b>Linear actuator (ML)</b>: the displacement is measured from the tail to the head; it can also be defined with two points, the tail and the head (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\" data-topic=\"actuators\">Actuators</a>).</li></ul>\n<p>The text of each one (the vector magnitude, the leader label and the actuator name) goes, by default, at the <b>midpoint</b> of the line; afterwards you can change it in its properties.</p>\n<p>You can choose the line and the end in any order, or select them before choosing the tool.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Midpoint line (M)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"mline\" title=\"Midpoint line\"></span> <b>Midpoint line (M)</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">Line dropdown</a>): tap the start point, which will be the <i>midpoint</i>, and then an end (or drag); the line grows equally to the other side.</p>\n<p>It gets a <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"midpoint\">midpoint</a> (M) constraint and the soft lock works as it does for lines.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Construction line (K)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"cline\" title=\"Construction line\"></span> <b>Construction line (K)</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">Line dropdown</a>): gray (#C3C3C3), \"thin\" weight, with a continuous stroke in the middle and a point at each end, separated by a gap; the points look like those of the \"dash-dot\" type.</p>\n<p>It is drawn like a normal line, with soft lock.</p>\n<p>It does not appear in the laser-cut SVG unless you change its class to Cut or Mark.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Circle (C)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"circle\" title=\"Circle\"></span> <b>Circle (C)</b>: center and a point on the edge.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Circle through 3 points</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"circle3\" title=\"Circle through 3 points\"></span> <b>Circle through 3 points</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">Circle dropdown</a>): tap 3 non-collinear points; the points end up on the circumference.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Ellipse (O)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"ellipse\" title=\"Ellipse\"></span> <b>Ellipse (O)</b>: tap the center; then tap (or drag to) the end of the first axis and, finally, a point of the second axis (the ellipse passes through the projection of that point onto the perpendicular to the first axis).</p>\n<p>You can also drag from the center to the end of the first axis.</p>\n<h4>What you can do with it</h4>\n<p>It is an element of its own:</p>\n<ul><li>It can be selected, copied and exported.</li><li>It appears in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"element-tree\">element tree</a>.</li><li>Its three points (center, axis 1 and axis 2) can be dimensioned and constrained.</li><li>It can be <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"trim-x\">trimmed</a>, you can <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"extend-e\">extend</a> up to it, and it supports coincident and tangent.</li><li>It does not support intersections.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Partial ellipse</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"earc\" title=\"Partial ellipse\"></span> <b>Partial ellipse</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">Ellipse dropdown</a>): creates an elliptical arc.</p>\n<p><b>First steps.</b> The first three steps are those of the ellipse (center, end of the first axis and point of the second axis); a dotted reference ellipse remains.</p>\n<p><b>Section.</b> Then <i>drag over it</i> from where the section starts to where it ends: the section follows the direction of your movement (↻ or ↺). You can also tap the start and then the end (with two taps the short section is taken).</p>\n<p><b>Ends.</b> The ends are points that slide over the ellipse.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Arc by center and ends (R)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"arc-c\" title=\"Arc by center and ends\"></span> <b>Arc by center and ends (R)</b>: center, start and end.</p>\n<p><b>Direction.</b> It comes from <i>where you move</i>: after tapping the start, move the cursor (or drag with your finger or the pencil) to the side you want and release at the end; the preview shows ↻ (clockwise) or ↺ (counterclockwise).</p>\n<p><b>Short arc.</b> With a direct tap at the end, the short arc is created.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Construction circle and arcs</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"ccircle\" title=\"Construction circle\"></span> <b>Construction circle and arcs</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">Circle and Arc dropdowns</a>): they use the color and weight of the construction line.</p>\n<p>The circle looks \"dash-dot\"; the arcs have a point at each end.</p>\n<p>You can also <i>convert</i> any chosen element to the \"Construction\" type in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"style\">Style panel</a> (it takes its color and weight).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Arc by 3 points (A)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"arc-3\" title=\"Arc by 3 points\"></span> <b>Arc by 3 points (A)</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">Arc dropdown</a>): start, intermediate point and end.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Spline (S)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"spline\" title=\"Spline\"></span> <b>Spline (S)</b>: tap the points the curve passes through.</p>\n<p>Tapping the last one again (or Esc) ends it, tapping the first one closes it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Rectangle (B)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"rect\" title=\"Rectangle\"></span> <b>Rectangle (B)</b>: tap (or drag between) two opposite corners.</p>\n<p>It gets horizontal and vertical constraints, so it stays a rectangle when you move a corner.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Center rectangle</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"rect-c\" title=\"Center rectangle\"></span> <b>Center rectangle</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">Rectangle dropdown</a>): tap the center and a corner (or drag from the center to the corner).</p>\n<p>The center is the midpoint of the diagonal.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Polygon (G)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"polygon\" title=\"Polygon\"></span> <b>Polygon (G)</b>: tap the center and the radius; then choose the number of sides (3 to 64) and whether it is <i>inscribed</i> (the radius reaches a vertex) or <i>circumscribed</i> (the radius reaches the middle of a side).</p>\n<p><b>Orientation.</b> The angle of the radius with the horizontal gives the orientation, with the same soft locks as lines.</p>\n<p><b>Regularity.</b> It stays regular when you move a vertex (vertices equidistant from the center and equal sides, hidden constraints).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Intersection</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"intersect\" title=\"Intersection\"></span> <b>Intersection</b>: it no longer has a button in the toolbar; it is still available in the panel (select 2 elements and press Intersection) and with the I key.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Soft lock and intersection magnet when drawing lines</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"soft-lock\" title=\"Soft lock\"></span> <b>Soft lock</b>: when drawing a line, if the cursor is near a notable direction, the line snaps to it and a dotted guide appears with a label.</p>\n<h4>Horizontal, vertical and 45°</h4>\n<p><b>Horizontal, vertical and 45°</b> (every 45°; the step can be changed in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"view\">View panel</a>, for example to 15°): the label shows H, V or the angle.</p>\n<p>H and V add their constraint; the other angles add none.</p>\n<h4>Near an existing line</h4>\n<p><b>Near an existing line</b> (within about 36 px of the cursor): <b>parallel (∥)</b> or <b>perpendicular (⟂)</b> to it, with the corresponding constraint.</p>\n<p>If the locked direction crosses a line, an arc or a circle near the cursor, the new line <b>ends at that crossing</b>, on that element (the perpendicular ends at the foot of the perpendicular).</p>\n<h4>Near an arc or circle</h4>\n<p><b>tangent (T)</b>, with the end on the curve. Also if you start on a curve.</p>\n<ul><li>If you start on a line or at its end, it also gives parallel and perpendicular to it.</li></ul>\n<h4>Automatic constraints</h4>\n<p>If the line ends up horizontal, vertical, perpendicular, parallel or tangent because you accepted the lock, the corresponding constraint is created with the elements involved (the end that falls on a line or curve stays on it).</p>\n<p>45° and the other intermediate angles add no constraint.</p>\n<ul><li>An <b>existing point</b>, an <b>intersection</b> or the <b>midpoint of a line</b> (marked with an M) near the cursor take priority over the lock. The midpoint creates the point with a hidden midpoint constraint, so it stays in the middle if the line changes. It is not offered on lines that are very short on screen.</li></ul>\n<h4>Intersection magnet</h4>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"magnet\" title=\"Magnet\"></span> <b>Intersection magnet</b>: if the start or the end of the line is near the intersection of two elements (lines, arcs, circles or splines), the line starts or ends there (∩) and the point stays on both.</p>\n<p>With the cursor, you have to stay close for a few milliseconds (80 by default, configurable in View; 0 = instantly); a tap or the start of a drag takes it instantly.</p>\n<p>If the constraint conflicts with the existing ones, it is removed and the line is left as is.</p>\n<p>The lock is turned off with <i>Soft lock</i> in the View panel.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "seleccionar": "<details data-ch=\"seleccionar\"><summary>Selecting and editing</summary>\n<p>Selecting (by tap, box or filter), viewing and changing an element's properties, and the tools that modify what you drew: copy, trim, extend, fillet and chamfer.</p>\n<details><summary>Select (V)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"select\" title=\"Select\"></span> <b>Select (V)</b> selects and drags points, lines or the edge of a circle (the mechanism moves according to the constraints).</p>\n<p><b>Selection box.</b> When you drag from an empty spot, it selects with a box. A box drawn from left to right takes what ends up entirely inside; from right to left, it also takes what it touches.</p>\n<p><b>Graphs.</b> <b>Graphs</b> are part of the selection: a single one behaves as if you tapped it; several, or together with other elements, are highlighted, deleted and moved along with them.</p>\n<h4>Several elements selected</h4>\n<p>With <b>several elements selected</b>, dragging any one of them moves all of them together: their free points (endpoints, centers, control points and the insertion point of texts).</p>\n<p><b>What does not move.</b> Fixed points and reference marks stay where they are, and the constraints still hold, so whatever is tied to something fixed adjusts instead of moving as a whole.</p>\n<p><b>A single element.</b> With a single element selected, dragging works as usual (the edge of a circle changes its radius).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Filter</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"filter\" title=\"Filter\"></span> <b>Filter</b> (in the Select <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">dropdown</a>) chooses what can be selected.</p>\n<ul><li><b>By entity:</b> points, lines, circles, arcs, ellipses, splines, texts, fills, dimensions.</li><li><b>By line type.</b></li></ul>\n<p><b>Scope.</b> It applies to tapping and to the selection box.</p>\n<p><b>Indicator.</b> A red dot on the button shows that a filter is active.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Box selection</summary>\n<p>With the Select tool, drag from an empty spot.</p>\n<ul><li><b>From left to right</b> (blue box, solid line): it selects the elements that end up <b>completely inside</b>.</li><li><b>From right to left</b> (orange box, dashed line): it also selects those the box <b>touches</b>.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Shift.</b> With Shift it adds to the selection.</p>\n<p><b>Scope.</b> It reaches points, lines, circles, arcs, splines and texts (a text by its insertion point).</p>\n<p><b>With a finger.</b> With a finger, turn on <i>Finger draws</i>, because otherwise the finger pans the canvas.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Overlapping elements</summary>\n<p><b>Overlapping elements</b> (Select tool) is for when several elements share a spot or sit almost on top of each other (coincident points, centers of concentric circles or arcs, collinear lines).</p>\n<h4>Opening the list</h4>\n<p><b>Tap and hold</b> on them for about 0.7 s: a floating window opens with a list of all the elements near the tap, from the nearest to the farthest. Check the ones you want: they are highlighted in orange in the drawing so you can identify them.</p>\n<h4>Window buttons</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Confirm:</b> leaves them selected (anything you already had selected outside that list is kept).</li><li><b>Cancel</b> (or Esc): closes without changing anything.</li><li><b>All</b> / <b>None:</b> checks the whole list.</li><li><b>Properties</b> (active when only one element is checked): leaves it selected and opens its properties window without closing the list; it is the way to edit it without an extra tap undoing the selection.</li></ul>\n<h4>Details</h4>\n<p><b>Moving the window.</b> You can move it by dragging its title bar, to clear the work area (it stays where you leave it).</p>\n<p><b>A single element.</b> With a single element under your finger it does not appear: the usual magnifier follows.</p>\n<p><b>Filter.</b> It respects the selection filter.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Properties</summary>\n<p><b>Properties:</b> <b>double-tap</b> a point, line, circle, arc, ellipse, spline, text or fill and its properties window opens (on a dimension, the double tap still edits its value).</p>\n<h4>What it shows depending on the element</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Any element:</b> <b>layer</b>, <b>color</b>, <b>line type</b>, <b>thickness</b> and <b>pattern scale</b>.</li><li><b>Text:</b> all its options: the content, the <b>font color</b> or <b>background color</b> (the same swatches; two radio buttons choose which one), the <b>font</b>, the <b>height</b>, the angle, <b>align with</b> a line, <b>readable</b> and the <b>insertion point</b>. If more texts are selected, the change applies to all of them.</li><li><b>Point:</b> its <b>color</b>, its X and Y coordinates, whether it is fixed and its <b><a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"reference-mark\">reference mark</a></b>.</li><li><b><a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"vector-w\">Vector</a>:</b> its <b>magnitude</b> (a value or a formula; typing it fixes it), whether it is fixed or read-only and its name.</li></ul>\n<h4>Constraints and dimensions</h4>\n<p>It also lists the element's <b>constraints and dimensions</b>, each with <b>Remove</b>, and offers the ones that can be added to that element alone:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Line:</b> horizontal, vertical, length and fix.</li><li><b>Circle:</b> diameter and fix.</li><li><b>Arc:</b> radius.</li></ul>\n<p>Changes apply immediately and can be undone.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Copy / Paste</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"copy\" title=\"Copy\"></span> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"paste\" title=\"Paste\"></span> <b>Copy / Paste</b> (in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">dropdown</a>; Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V): the button shows Copy, and after copying it switches to showing Paste (to copy again, use Ctrl+C or the dropdown).</p>\n<p><b>Copy</b> stores the selection with its internal constraints.</p>\n<p><b>Paste</b> asks for a point and places the center of the box that encloses what you copied at that point (you see the box as you move). You can paste several times; Esc or Select ends it.</p>\n<p><b>Retention.</b> What you copied is kept even if you delete the original, but it is lost when you close the app.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Trim (X)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"trim\" title=\"Trim\"></span> <b>Trim (X)</b>: press and hold, then drag over the sections you want to remove (between crossings with other elements).</p>\n<p><b>One section.</b> A tap trims one section. Only that section is removed: the rest of the element is not split at the other crossings. With no crossings, it deletes the whole element.</p>\n<h4>Ellipses and splines</h4>\n<p>It also trims <b>ellipses</b> and <b>splines</b>:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Full ellipse:</b> it needs 2 crossings and becomes an elliptical arc.</li><li><b>Elliptical arc:</b> it is shortened or split into two that share center and axes.</li><li><b>Spline:</b> each piece is a new spline that passes through the cut, through the original points and through extra points taken from the curve to keep the shape; it is a very close approximation, not an exact one.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Extend (E)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"extend\" title=\"Extend\"></span> <b>Extend (E)</b>: tap a segment or arc near the end you want to lengthen; it reaches the first element it finds, including ellipses, elliptical arcs and splines.</p>\n<p><b>Open spline.</b> An open spline can also be extended: it is lengthened in a straight line, in the direction of its tangent at that end, and the new end point lands on the element it finds (a spline with relations is not extended).</p>\n<p><b>Extending again.</b> When you extend again, the same end moves on to the next crossing (it leaves no points at the previous ones).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Fillet (F) and Chamfer</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"fillet\" title=\"Fillet\"></span> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"chamfer\" title=\"Chamfer\"></span> <b>Fillet (F) and Chamfer</b> (button after <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"polygon-g\">Polygon</a>; tap again or press and hold to choose) joins <b>two stroke elements</b> with a tangent arc (fillet) or with a line (chamfer).</p>\n<p><b>What can be joined.</b> Segments, arcs and elliptical arcs, in any combination; construction lines do not count.</p>\n<h4>Three ways to choose them</h4>\n<ol><li>Tap the point of a <b>corner</b> (where two elements end, or two points joined by a coincidence constraint) and type the <b>radius</b> or the chamfer <b>distance</b>.</li><li>Tap one element and then the other, <b>on the side you want to keep</b>, even if they do not meet at a corner. If they cross or do not even touch, the fillet lengthens or trims each one up to its tangent point; the chamfer needs them to intersect, either themselves or their extensions.</li><li><b>Select before choosing the tool</b> one or several corner points (the same value for all of them, for example the four of a rectangle) or exactly two elements (the part toward the end farthest from the vertex is kept).</li></ol>\n<h4>The value</h4>\n<p>The value can be a formula (<code>R1/2</code>, <code>m*0.3</code>). Of the possible solutions, the arc smaller than a half turn and closest to the vertex wins, regardless of the order in which you choose the elements.</p>\n<h4>Dimensions that remain</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Fillet:</b> it is an arc with its <b>radius dimension</b> (editable: changing it moves the arc; in the arc's properties, the <b>Radius</b> button edits that same dimension).</li><li><b>Chamfer:</b> it carries one <b>dimension per leg</b> (D1, D2...; they can be different), measured in a straight line from the vertex.</li></ul>\n<h4>The vertex</h4>\n<p>At a corner of two lines that share the point, <b>the point is not deleted</b>: it stays as a virtual vertex on the two straight lines, and the dimensions and constraints that used it still hold. Between other elements, the vertex (if they intersect) stays as a hidden virtual point.</p>\n<p><b>Trimming segments.</b> When a segment is trimmed this way, its length dimensions and its length equality are removed (they no longer hold) and you are notified.</p>\n<h4>Warnings and limits</h4>\n<ul><li>With loose elements, changing the radius may let the solver deform them: it is best to fix or dimension whatever must not move.</li><li>If the value does not fit, you are warned.</li><li>A fill that used the two elements includes the arc or the chamfer.</li><li>Circles, full ellipses (trim the ellipse first) and vectors cannot be filleted.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Locked texts and images</summary>\n<p><b>Locked texts and images</b>: a tap <b>does not select a text</b> (in documents with many texts they were grabbed and moved by accident).</p>\n<h4>Editing a text in place</h4>\n<p>A tap <b>edits it in place</b>, with the same input as the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\" data-topic=\"write-a-text-t\">Text</a> tool placed over it and the cursor where you tapped (an equation without its <code>$$</code>, a normal text as it is, a script with its code).</p>\n<ul><li><b>Enter</b> saves (in a script, Shift+Enter).</li><li><b>Shift+Enter</b> makes a new line.</li><li><b>Tapping outside</b> also saves.</li><li><b>Esc</b> leaves it as it was.</li></ul>\n<p>If what you typed gives an error, you are warned and the input stays open so you can fix it.</p>\n<p><b>Collapsed code object.</b> A collapsed code object (an inserted library, or a script without \"Show the code in the drawing\") is not edited this way: the tap passes through to whatever is underneath (a line, a point); it is opened with a double tap, in its properties.</p>\n<h4>Selecting a text</h4>\n<p>To select a text, <b>enclose it with a box</b>, dragging from it or from an empty spot (from left to right it must end up entirely inside; from right to left, touching it is enough); once selected, you drag it to move it.</p>\n<p><b>Double tap and links.</b> The <b>double tap</b> still opens its properties, and a tap on a <b>link</b> selects the text and shows the pill with its destination (without opening it).</p>\n<h4>Images</h4>\n<p>An image has an <b>insertion point</b>, like a text: a real point that shows when you select it, accepts constraints and can be read in formulas (<code>p12.x</code>). In its properties you choose its anchor (9 positions), and when you enlarge or shrink it that point stays fixed.</p>\n<p><b>Locked image.</b> A <b>locked image</b> (\"Locked\" checkbox in its properties) follows the same rule: tapping and dragging ignore it, and the double tap opens its properties so you can unlock it.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "dividir": "<details data-ch=\"dividir\"><summary>Splitting, copying and repeating</summary>\n<p>Splitting an element into sections and repeating geometry with linear arrays, circular arrays and mirror copies, linked to the original or not.</p>\n<details><summary>Split</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"split\" title=\"Split\"></span> <b>Split</b> (panel, Dimensions section...): with 1 segment, arc or circle plus the points that are on it, it cuts it at those points.</p>\n<p>With no points, it divides it into <b>n equal parts</b>:</p>\n<ul><li>On lines, the parts get an equal-length constraint.</li><li>On arcs and circles there is no equal-angle constraint.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Linear array</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"array\" title=\"Linear array\"></span> <b>Linear array</b>: select what you want to copy.</p>\n<p><b>Direction 1.</b> With a line, it goes from its start to its end; with no line, it is horizontal.</p>\n<p><b>Spacing and number.</b> Define the spacing and the <i>total number</i> (it includes the original; if you only give the spacing, the total is 2).</p>\n<p><b>Direction 2.</b> It is optional and fills a grid; with no line it is perpendicular to 1.</p>\n<p><b>Highlighting.</b> The chosen directions are highlighted with an arrow on the canvas.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Circular array</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"array-circ\" title=\"Circular array\"></span> <b>Circular array</b>: it is defined with this data:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Center</b>: the origin or a point you tap.</li><li><b>Total angle</b>: 360° by default.</li><li><b>Total number</b>.</li><li><b>Equal spacing</b> or <b>angle between instances</b>.</li><li><b>Reversed direction</b>.</li><li><i>Skip instances</i>: for example 3, 5.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Variables in Split and in the arrays</summary>\n<p>The fields of these tools (number of parts, spacing, total number, angles and skipped instances) accept dimensions and the <code>x:value</code> variables defined in the texts, as well as numbers and formulas.</p>\n<h4>Integer fields</h4>\n<p>In the fields that ask for an <b>integer</b> (number of parts, total number, skipped instances), a variable or formula is rounded to the nearest integer, like <code>round(variable;0)</code>; a plain number must be an integer.</p>\n<h4>Variables menu</h4>\n<p>The dialogs have a <b>variables menu</b> with a filter (all, dimensions, angles, text variables): choosing one inserts its name into the field where you were typing, at the cursor position.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Mirror copy</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"mirror\" title=\"Mirror copy\"></span> <b>Mirror copy</b>: select what you want to reflect, press the button and tap the line that acts as the axis.</p>\n<p>Points on the axis are shared.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Linked copies</summary>\n<p><b>Linked copies</b> (option \"Link the copies to the original\", on by default): each point of the copy is tied to its original with automatic constraints, so the copy is a rigid replica that <b>follows the original</b> if you move it, rotate it or change its size.</p>\n<h4>Constraints by tool</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Linear array</b>: the distance and the direction (the angle relative to the chosen line, or absolute if there is no line).</li><li><b>Circular array</b>: equal distance to the center and the angle at the center.</li><li><b>Mirror copy</b>: symmetry about the axis and, for circles, equal radius.</li></ul>\n<h4>Array dimensions</h4>\n<p><b>The array dimensions are optional</b> (\"Show the array dimensions\"):</p>\n<ul><li>In the linear array, the distance between the original and the first copy.</li><li>In the circular one, the angular dimension.</li></ul>\n<h4>Without linking</h4>\n<p>With the option off, the copies are independent and carry only their internal constraints.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "restricciones": "<details data-ch=\"restricciones\"><summary>Constraints</summary>\n<p>The geometric relations the solver always maintains: horizontal, parallel, tangent, coincident and the rest, with their numbering in the drawing.</p>\n<p>Select the elements and press the button. Each button states what selection it needs.</p>\n<details><summary>Horizontal / Vertical</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-h\" title=\"Horizontal\"></span> <b>Horizontal</b> and <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-v\" title=\"Vertical\"></span> <b>Vertical</b> apply to lines, or to 2 points.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Distribute ↔ / Distribute ↕</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-dsh\" title=\"Distribute ↔\"></span> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-dsv\" title=\"Distribute ↕\"></span> <b>Distribute ↔ / Distribute ↕</b> apply to 3 or more points or texts (a text, by its insertion point).</p>\n<p><b>Result.</b> They end up <b>evenly spaced</b> horizontally (x) or vertically (y), between the two at the ends (the leftmost and the rightmost, or the lowest and the highest), which do not move when you apply it.</p>\n<p><b>The other coordinate.</b> It stays free: to align them as well, use Horizontal or Vertical.</p>\n<p><b>Behavior.</b> It is a constraint like the others: the spacing is an unknown, so if you drag an end point the ones in between are spread out again, and if you drag one in between they all move.</p>\n<p><b>Label.</b> It is labeled ↔1, ↕1 next to each point.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Parallel / Perpendicular</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-par\" title=\"Parallel\"></span> <b>Parallel</b> and <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-perp\" title=\"Perpendicular\"></span> <b>Perpendicular</b> apply to 2 or more lines.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Coincident</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-coi\" title=\"Coincident\"></span> <b>Coincident</b> accepts three selections:</p>\n<ul><li><b>2 or more points:</b> they end up in the same place.</li><li><b>Point(s) and 1 line, arc, circle, ellipse or spline:</b> the points end up on the element.</li><li><b>Lines or arcs and 1 ellipse or spline:</b> the nearest end of each line or arc ends up on the curve.</li></ul>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-col\" title=\"Collinear\"></span> <b>Collinear</b>: 2 or more lines.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Midpoint</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-mid\" title=\"Midpoint\"></span> <b>Midpoint</b> applies to 1 point + 1 line.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Concentric (◎)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-conc\" title=\"Concentric\"></span> <b>Concentric</b> applies to 2 or more circles or arcs.</p>\n<p><b>Which rules.</b> The center of the first one rules and the other centers coincide with it.</p>\n<p><b>If they already coincide.</b> If they already share the center, you are warned and nothing is added.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Equal</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"equal\" title=\"Equal\"></span> <b>Equal</b> applies to 2 lines (equal length) or to 2 circles/arcs (equal radius).</p>\n<p><b>Fillet and chamfer.</b> When you fillet or chamfer a corner, the length equality of the lines that get shortened becomes an equality of distances between their other end and the virtual vertex of the corner (it shows as \"=\" next to those points).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Tangent</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"con-tan\" title=\"Tangent\"></span> <b>Tangent</b> accepts these selections:</p>\n<ul><li>Line + circle/arc.</li><li>2 circles/arcs.</li><li>A spline with a line, circle or arc.</li><li>An <b>ellipse</b> (or elliptical arc) with a line, circle or arc.</li><li>2 ellipses.</li></ul>\n<p>With ellipses and splines the contact point appears.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Fix / release</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"lock\" title=\"Fix / release\"></span> <b>Fix / release</b> applies to points, lines or circles.</p>\n<p><b>Effect.</b> A fixed point does not move when you drag.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Reference mark</summary>\n<p><b>Reference mark</b> (properties of a point, <b>Reference mark at</b> and the line): a mark <b>is not a fixed point</b>.</p>\n<h4>How it works</h4>\n<p>The solver does not move it while solving, but after each change it is <b>projected onto its line</b>, which is already in its new position and orientation. This way it slides along the line by itself, carried by it, and <b>remembers the distance traveled</b>: the distance between the mark and another point on the line accumulates the path.</p>\n<p><b>Versus a position dimension.</b> A position dimension cannot do this, because it returns to the same value when a trip is closed; the mark can.</p>\n<p><b>Appearance.</b> It is drawn as an <b>x</b>.</p>\n<h4>Reset marks</h4>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"mark-reset\" title=\"Reset marks\"></span> <b>Reset marks</b> (button in the Spline group, or <b>Reset</b> in the point's properties) returns each mark to its <i>reference</i>: the distance along the line, from its first end, that it had when it was assigned.</p>\n<p><b>What it is for.</b> It is useful for placing the mechanism somewhere else and starting the reading again from zero.</p>\n<p><b>Take the current one.</b> <b>Take the current one</b> changes the reference.</p>\n<h4>Uses</h4>\n<p>It is useful for rails, wheels that roll on a rotating line and planimeters.</p>\n<p><b>Example, the polar planimeter.</b> The pole and the arm with the tracer P follow the outline of a figure; the rail (perpendicular to the arm) passes through P and carries a mark.</p>\n<ul><li>D = distance from the mark to P (read-only).</li><li>The wheel's rotation is <code>(D-D0)/(O1/2)</code>.</li><li>The area is <code>arm length × radius × rotation</code>.</li></ul>\n<h4>Rules and limit</h4>\n<ul><li>A mark belongs to a single line and cannot be an end of it.</li><li>If the line is deleted, it stops being a mark.</li><li>Copies and arrays reassign it to the copy of its line.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Limit.</b> The mark advances in steps (each solve), so the accumulated travel has an error that decreases with finer drags (in the planimeter, 2 to 4 % with a normal drag).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Indicators</summary>\n<p>Each relation is labeled with its letter and number next to the elements it involves (the same number and letter on all of them).</p>\n<ul><li><b>A point with a line, circle or arc:</b> next to the point and the element.</li><li><b>A horizontal or vertical between 2 points:</b> next to both.</li><li><b>Gear:</b> marked with <b>G</b>.</li><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"rack\" title=\"Rack\"></span> <b><a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\" data-topic=\"rack\">Rack</a>:</b> wheel-and-rack ones are marked with <b>Cr</b>.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Redundant constraints</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"redundant\" title=\"Redundant\"></span> A redundant constraint adds no information (others already imply the same thing) and slows down every calculation, because the solver solves twice and diagnoses them.</p>\n<p><b>Removing them.</b> The <b>Redundant</b> button (Constraints group) lists them and offers to remove them; when you open a file that has them, a notice appears.</p>\n<p><b>Named dimensions.</b> Named dimensions are not removed automatically (they may feed formulas).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Numbering</summary>\n<p>Each constraint carries a number per type (H1, H2, ⟂1, ∥1, =1, ●1, T1...).</p>\n<p><b>Several elements.</b> Those that involve several elements share a single number and are labeled next to each element (for example, two perpendicular lines each show ⟂1).</p>\n<p><b>Reuse.</b> The number is not reused if you delete the constraint.</p>\n<p><b>Element tree.</b> In the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"element-tree\">element tree</a> each constraint appears with its number: tap it to select it together with its elements and bring the canvas to it.</p>\n<p><b>Automatic ones.</b> The automatic constraints that place a point on an element while drawing (●) are not labeled.</p>\n<p><b>Contradictions.</b> If a constraint contradicts the others, the app warns you and undoes the action.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "cotas": "<details data-ch=\"cotas\"><summary>Dimensions</summary>\n<p>Dimensions: measurements that drive the geometry or only read it, with formulas, names, scale and units.</p>\n<p>The full list of functions and operators, with examples, is in <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\">Functions and operators</a>.</p>\n<details><summary>Dimension (D)</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dim-dist\" title=\"Dimension\"></span> <b>Dimension (D)</b> (after Text): a single tool for all dimensions.</p>\n<p>Tap the elements and the dimension is created automatically once the selection is complete:</p>\n<ul><li><b>2 points</b> or <b>a point and a line</b>: the distance.</li><li><b>2 parallel lines</b> (less than 1° apart): the distance between them.</li><li><b>2 non-parallel lines</b>: the angle.</li><li><b>An arc</b>: the radius.</li><li><b>A circle</b>: the diameter.</li><li><b>An arc or circle with another element</b> (a point, a line, another arc or circle, or an ellipse): the distance from that element to the center.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Arc or circle against another element.</b> Tap the other element first and then the arc or circle (an arc alone, tapped first, gives its radius), or select them before choosing the tool.</p>\n<p><b>With a previous selection.</b> If something was already selected when you chose Dimension, it dimensions that selection (a single line gives its length).</p>\n<p><b>Removing from the selection.</b> Tap an element again to remove it; Esc clears the selection.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Distance</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dim-dist\" title=\"Distance\"></span> <b>Distance</b> dimensions these cases:</p>\n<ul><li>2 points.</li><li>1 line.</li><li>Point + line.</li><li><b>Arc or circle + another element</b>: distance from that element to the center.</li><li><b>2 parallel lines</b>: perpendicular distance between them.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Parallel lines.</b> If they did not have the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"parallel--perpendicular\">Parallel</a> constraint, it is added, unless the dimension is a reference one. The lines must be parallel within 1°.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Angle</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dim-ang\" title=\"Angle\"></span> <b>Angle</b> dimensions 2 lines.</p>\n<p><b>Value with no limit.</b> 250° or 400° (one full turn and 40°) are valid; see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"angles-without-limit\">Angles without limit</a> below.</p>\n<p><b>Sector.</b> Where you place the dimension decides which sector is dimensioned (for example 20° or 160°). Drag the dimension to reposition it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Radius / ⌀</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dim-rad\" title=\"Radius / ⌀\"></span> <b>Radius / ⌀</b> dimensions an arc (R) or a circle (diameter, with the ⌀ symbol).</p>\n<p>They follow the ANSI standard:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Text inside:</b> the dimension line runs through the interior.</li><li><b>Text outside:</b> a radial leader with the arrow touching from outside.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Scaled dimension</summary>\n<p><b>Scaled dimension</b> (distances, radii and diameters): type <code>value[*k]</code> in the dimension's value, e.g. <code>4[*10]</code>.</p>\n<p><b>What it measures and what it says.</b> The <b>drawing measures value × k</b> (here 40 units) and the <b>dimension says the value</b> (\"4\"), <u>underlined</u> as in ISO 129 / UNE 1-039. With <code>4[*0.1]</code> the drawing measures 0.4 and the dimension still says \"4\".</p>\n<p><b>Formulas.</b> The value can be a formula (<code>D1/2[*10]</code>) and <b>so can the scale</b>: a number, a text variable (<code>30[*k]</code> with <code>k:25</code> in a text) or a formula with dimensions (<code>4[*2*D1]</code>). If the variable changes, the drawing is recalculated and the dimension still says 30.</p>\n<p><b>Read-only dimensions.</b> When you check \"Read-only\" the <b>Scale (×)</b> field appears (empty = no scale) and the dimension says the <b>measurement ÷ scale</b>, underlined and in italics (a measurement of 13.333 with scale 10 says \"1.333\").</p>\n<p><b>Where it shows.</b> The scale is shown when you edit the dimension (<code>value[*k]</code>, or in its field) and disappears when you delete it.</p>\n<p><b>Limit.</b> It does not apply to angles.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Dimension with unit</summary>\n<p><b>Dimension with unit</b> (distances, radii and diameters): type the value with its unit, <code>126'in</code>.</p>\n<p><b>What it measures and what it says.</b> The <b>drawing measures the real thing</b> and the <b>dimension says it in its unit</b>: \"126 in\".</p>\n<ul><li>A length is measured in mm: <code>126'in</code> measures 3200.4.</li><li>Another SI quantity: <code>5'kN</code> measures 5000, <code>12'V</code> measures 12.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Changing the unit.</b> In the dimension's window, <b>Dimension unit</b> shows and changes the unit:</p>\n<ul><li>With a number it converts it: <code>126'in</code> becomes <code>3200,4'mm</code>.</li><li>With a formula it only changes the unit at the end: <code>D2*2'ft</code>.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Formula with no unit at the end.</b> It says the unit of a dimension in the formula with the same quantity (<code>D2*2</code> with D2 in in says in) or the SI one.</p>\n<p><b>Seeing it smaller or larger.</b> Use the scale: <code>126'in[*0.1]</code> draws 320.04 and the dimension still says \"126 in\", underlined.</p>\n<p><b>Read-only.</b> In a <b>read-only</b> dimension, the unit only changes the label (a measurement of 254 mm, with the unit in, says \"10 in\").</p>\n<h4>Scale with units</h4>\n<p><b>Scale with units</b> (representation scale): for a quantity that is not a length, the scale carries units and the quantity times the scale gives the drawing length.</p>\n<p><b>Example.</b> <code>30'kN[*1'mm/kN]</code> measures 30 mm and the dimension says \"30 kN\", <b>not underlined</b> (it is not out of scale, it is at the scale you declared).</p>\n<p><b>Scale as a variable.</b> The scale can be a text variable, <code>eF:1'mm/kN</code>, and each dimension or vector uses it with <code>[*eF]</code>. It reads the eF defined earlier in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\" data-topic=\"reading-order\">reading order</a>, so if you redefine it further down (<code>eF:10'mm/kN</code>) the scale of everything below changes.</p>\n<p><b>Warnings.</b> If the quantity times the scale does not give a length (<code>30'kN[*2'mm]</code>) it warns with ⚠. Without a scale, <code>30'kN</code> measures 30000 (in SI) and the app warns you.</p>\n<p><b>Read-only with another quantity.</b> A <b>read-only</b> dimension with a unit of another quantity (kN) needs a scale with units to read the measurement: it says \"⚠ kN\" until it has one. With it, it says the measurement divided by the scale (45 mm with <code>1'mm/kN</code> says \"45 kN\").</p>\n<h4>Dimensions with unit in formulas</h4>\n<p>In formulas and calculation texts a dimension with a unit enters as a <b>quantity</b>: what its label says, with its quantity and without the scale.</p>\n<p><b>Example.</b> With D2 = 126 in and D4 = 20 in, <code>A:D2*D4=</code> gives the area (in m²; <code>A='ft^2</code> asks for it in ft²).</p>\n<p><b>Dimension without a unit.</b> It still enters as a number: to add it to one with a unit, give it the unit in the formula, <code>D1'mm+D2</code> (<code>D1+D2</code> gives ⚠).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Read-only (reference)</summary>\n<p>It measures and shows the value in italics without constraining the geometry.</p>\n<ul><li>Tap a dimension once to <b>select</b> it (for example to delete it), <b>drag</b> it to reposition it, and <b>tap twice</b> to edit its value.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Formulas</summary>\n<p>Any dimension can take an expression: <code>3π/4</code>, <code>2π*5</code>, <code>10*sin(30)</code>, <code>10+1/3</code>, <code>√2</code>, <code>2^3</code>. The same applies inside the <code>expr=</code> outputs and the <code>x:...</code> assignments of the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\" data-topic=\"assign-and-show-values\">texts</a>.</p>\n<h4>Ranges</h4>\n<p><b>Ranges</b> are vectors of evenly spaced numbers:</p>\n<ul><li><code>x:start..end;n</code> defines <code>x</code> with <code>n</code> elements between <code>start</code> and <code>end</code> (both included).</li><li><code>x:start..step..end</code> defines <code>start</code>, <code>start+step</code>... without going past <code>end</code> (the step can be negative).</li></ul>\n<p>Each part can be a formula. <code>x=</code> shows the elements; a vector cannot yet be used inside a numeric formula (it warns).</p>\n<h4>Operators and constants</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Operators</b>: <code>+ - * / ^</code> (also <code>×</code>, <code>÷</code>, <code>**</code>) and implicit multiplication (<code>2π</code>, <code>3(4+1)</code>).</li><li><b>Constants</b>: <code>π</code> (or <code>pi</code>) and <code>e</code>.</li></ul>\n<h4>Single-value functions</h4>\n<ul><li><code>sin</code> (or <code>sen</code>), <code>cos</code>, <code>tan</code>, <code>sec</code>, <code>csc</code>, <code>cot</code>: in degrees, or in radians if the argument contains π or is an angle.</li><li><code>asin</code>, <code>acos</code>, <code>atan</code>: return degrees.</li><li><code>sinh</code>, <code>cosh</code>, <code>tanh</code>, <code>asinh</code>, <code>acosh</code>, <code>atanh</code>.</li><li><code>sqrt</code> (or <code>raiz</code>, <code>√</code>), <code>abs</code>, <code>exp</code>, <code>ln</code>, <code>log</code> (or <code>log10</code>).</li><li><code>deg(x)</code> (radians to degrees) and <code>rad(x)</code> (degrees to radians).</li><li><code>floor</code> (<code>piso</code>), <code>ceil</code> (<code>techo</code>), <code>trunc</code> and <code>sign</code> (<code>signo</code>).</li></ul>\n<h4>Multi-value functions</h4>\n<p>The values are separated with <code>;</code> (or with a comma followed by a space or after a variable; <code>2,5</code> is the number 2.5):</p>\n<ul><li><code>round(x; n)</code> (<code>redondear</code>): <code>n</code> decimals, 0 if omitted; halves round away from zero.</li><li><code>min(a; b; ...)</code>, <code>max(a; b; ...)</code>.</li><li><code>clamp(x; mínimo; máximo)</code>.</li><li><code>mod(a; b)</code>: the result takes the sign of <code>b</code>.</li><li><code>hypot(a; b; ...)</code>.</li><li><code>atan2(y; x)</code>: degrees.</li></ul>\n<h4>Comparisons</h4>\n<p><code>&lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= == !=</code> (also <code>≤ ≥ ≠</code>) give 1 if true and 0 if not (<code>==</code> and <code>!=</code> tolerate rounding).</p>\n<p><code>if(condición; si_verdadero; si_falso)</code> (or <code>si</code>) chooses between two values: it only calculates the chosen branch, so <code>if(D1==0; 0; 10/D1)</code> does not fail.</p>\n<h4>Reserved names</h4>\n<p>The names of these functions cannot be used as a dimension or variable name, nor can <code>t</code> (time), <code>j</code>, <code>ω</code>, <code>θ</code> or <code>α</code> (only those lowercase letters). They are reserved for future engines, animations and dynamic calculations; for now they have no value.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Dimension names and formulas between dimensions</summary>\n<p>Each dimension has an automatic name by type:</p>\n<ul><li><b>D</b>: distance.</li><li><b>A</b>: angle.</li><li><b>R</b>: arc radius.</li><li><b>O</b>: circle diameter.</li></ul>\n<p>This gives D1, D2, A1, R1, O1...; the number is not reused.</p>\n<p><b>Where to see and change it.</b> You see it in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"element-tree\">element tree</a> and when you tap the dimension twice, where you can also <b>change it</b> (letters, numbers and _; the new name is updated in the formulas that use it).</p>\n<p><b>Using it in formulas.</b> A dimension's name can be used in another's formula: <code>D1*sin(3π/4)</code>, <code>D1/2+1</code>, <code>2*O1</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Recalculation.</b> When you change a dimension's value, the ones that depend on it are recalculated automatically and the geometry is solved again. If the result is impossible (a conflict, or a distance or radius that would end up zero or negative) everything is undone and you are warned.</p>\n<h4>Rules</h4>\n<ul><li>An <b>angle</b> enters the formula as its measure in radians (like π): <code>sin(A1)</code> works and <code>R1*A1</code> is the arc length.</li><li><b>Read-only</b> (reference) dimensions can be read, see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"read-only-dimensions-as-inputs\">below</a>.</li><li>There can be no cycles between driving dimensions (D1 depends on D2 and D2 on D1).</li><li>A diameter (O) is worth the diameter, not the radius.</li><li>If a dimension that another depended on is deleted, the latter keeps its current value and the app warns you.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Read-only dimensions as inputs</summary>\n<p>The formula of a driving dimension can read a reference dimension, that is, a measurement of the geometry (the length of a line, an angle, a radius). See <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"conceptos\" data-topic=\"driving-and-reference-dimensions\">Driving and reference dimensions</a>.</p>\n<h4>How it is solved</h4>\n<p>Since the measurement depends on the geometry and the geometry on the dimension, the app <b>solves and recalculates in rounds</b> until the formula matches the measurement (secant method, and if it does not close, damped repetition).</p>\n<p>This happens when editing a dimension, when creating one and <b>while you drag</b>, so the output dimension moves with the drawing.</p>\n<p><b>Fixed points.</b> So that the output does not push the input, <b>the points measured by the read-only dimensions that the formulas read are kept fixed</b> while solving (in an involute, the chord D1 = R·A1 gets longer without moving the tangent point that A1 measures). If the drawing needs them to move, the app retries without fixing them.</p>\n<p><b>While dragging.</b> When you drag a mechanism with formulas that read measurements, a point on a circle that the mouse cannot reach exactly is pulled in on its own in the first calculation pass.</p>\n<p><b>If it does not stabilize.</b> For example, a dimension defined as its own measurement + 1: it is undone and you are warned.</p>\n<h4>Example: the wheel that rolls without slipping</h4>\n<p><b>The tangent point T governs</b> (the input): D1 (read-only) measures the distance from the origin to T and A1 (driving, in radians) is worth <code>D1/(O1/2)</code>.</p>\n<p>When you drag T along the ground, the wheel turns as much as it advances and the marked point P, which stays as the output, traces the cycloid, over several turns.</p>\n<p><b>What is best.</b> It is best for the input to be the most stable measurement (the position of T) and the output the calculated one. Doing it the other way round (dragging P and having the distance read the angle) works, but is ill-conditioned near the ground.</p>\n<h4>Limit</h4>\n<p>A very fast drag may stop converging at some step (the last good state is kept).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Angles without limit</summary>\n<p>An angular dimension no longer stays between 0° and 180°.</p>\n<ul><li>Type 250° and the geometry ends up at 250° (the arc is drawn beyond 180°).</li><li>400° is one full turn and 40° (the arc shows the 40°).</li></ul>\n<p><b>Direction.</b> It is measured in the direction (counterclockwise or clockwise) in which the dimension was created.</p>\n<p><b>Read-only.</b> A <b>read-only</b> angular dimension follows the geometry without jumping: going from 180° to 190°, to 360° and to 400° it keeps growing, and on the way back it goes down again. To do this it takes the equivalent closest to the last value, so it stays correct as long as each change is less than half a turn.</p>\n<p><b>Old drawings.</b> They keep their 0° to 180° value until you edit that dimension (by tapping it twice) or drag it to another sector, and from then on it is continuous.</p>\n<p><b>In formulas.</b> An angle enters in radians, also beyond 2π.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Degrees or radians</summary>\n<p>Angular dimensions have a unit selector.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Degrees:</b> sin/cos/tan use degrees (radians if the argument contains π).</li><li><b>Radians:</b> everything is in radians.</li></ul>\n<p>Dimension formulas always read degrees; the <b>calculation texts</b> and the graphs follow the <b>Settings → Angles in</b> option (radians in new drawings, degrees in earlier ones).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Point color</summary>\n<p>Tap a point twice and choose its color in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"properties\">properties</a> window (palette or free color; \"auto\" goes back to the normal color).</p>\n<p>The color is only visible on screen: points do not appear in exports.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Free label</summary>\n<p><b>Free label</b>: at the end of a dimension's value, <code>[\"texto\"]</code> changes what it says: <code>50[\"D\"]</code> measures 50 and drives the drawing, but reads \"D\".</p>\n<p><b>With a formula.</b> With <code>[$fórmula$]</code> or <code>[$$fórmula$$]</code> the label is drawn with the text engine (subscripts, Greek letters, fractions): <code>50[$D/2$]</code>, <code>8[$r_1$]</code>.</p>\n<p><b>With a scale.</b> It combines with the scale, in that order: <code>30[*1'mm/kN][\"F\"]</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Editing and removing.</b> When you edit the dimension it is shown in full; to remove it, delete the bracket.</p>\n<p><b>Export.</b> In the SVG and the DXF a label with a formula comes out as plain text.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Dimension font size</summary>\n<p><b>Dimension font size</b>: with <b>Settings → Dimensions use the text font size</b> (on in new documents), the dimension font is measured like that of the texts.</p>\n<ul><li><b>On:</b> in <b>points</b> with a page and in drawing units without one; dimensions grow and shrink with the zoom along with the drawing, also when exporting.</li><li><b>Unchecked</b> (earlier drawings): the font is in screen pixels and does not change with the zoom.</li></ul>\n<h4>Where to choose it</h4>\n<ul><li>In the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"style\">Style</a> panel, under <b>Dimension text</b>: with no dimensions selected it changes the document's, which all dimensions without their own follow; with dimensions selected, theirs.</li><li>In each dimension's window (double tap), under <b>Dimension text</b>.</li></ul>\n<h4>Arrows</h4>\n<p>Arrows and spacing grow with the font. Arrowheads follow the ASME Y14.2 standard: their length is the height of the digits and their base is a third of the length (3:1), filled. Leaders use the same arrow as the document's dimensions.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Leaders</summary>\n<p><b>Leaders</b> (annotations): with the dimension tool (or the <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dim-dist\" title=\"Distance\"></span> Distance button), choose <b>a point and the element it belongs to</b>; its window opens. With a line, it is the same gesture as for the vector and the linear actuator: the chosen end is the <b>head</b> (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"head-and-tail-one-gesture\">Head and tail</a>).</p>\n<h4>Depending on the chosen point</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Endpoint</b> of a line, an arc, an elliptical arc or an open spline: <b>the element itself becomes the leader</b>. The arrow goes at that endpoint, in the direction of the curve; its color, line type and thickness are the element's (change them in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"style\">Style</a> panel, with the leader or the element selected), and the element does not appear in the laser-cut SVG.</li><li><b>Center</b> of a circle or arc, or an <b>intermediate point</b>: a straight line of its own is drawn with the arrow at the point and the panel's style.</li></ul>\n<h4>The label</h4>\n<p>The <b>label is a text of the drawing</b>: its height is in drawing units, like that of the texts, and its arrow is the same as the document's dimensions.</p>\n<p>In its window (when creating it and with a double tap) you choose:</p>\n<ul><li>The label (plain text, <code>\"texto\"</code>, <code>$fórmula$</code> or <code>$$fórmula$$</code>).</li><li><b>Where it goes</b>: on a line, an arc or an elliptical arc, an endpoint or the midpoint (by default, the midpoint, beside the line); on a spline, any of its points.</li><li>The <b>insertion point</b> of its box (corners, midpoints or center, as in the texts; it is kept a quarter of its height away from the point).</li><li>The <b>font</b> and the <b>height</b> (with a page, in points, like the texts; it starts with the font and height of new texts).</li></ul>\n<p><b>Dragging.</b> You can also drag the label: it jumps to the nearest allowed place. If the geometry moves, it goes with it.</p>\n<p><b>What it does not do.</b> It constrains nothing: the solver does not see it, and it is deleted with its point or its element (deleting it leaves the element).</p>\n<p><b>Export.</b> It appears in the SVG and the DXF, with the formula drawn.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "splines": "<details data-ch=\"splines\"><summary>Splines and paths (cams and trajectories)</summary>\n<p>Free curves that pass through points of the drawing, the path of a moving point and offset curves, the basis of cams and trajectories.</p>\n<p>A spline is a <b>cubic B-spline</b> that passes through real points of the drawing.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Open:</b> zero curvature at the ends.</li><li><b>Closed:</b> periodic, smooth all the way around, with no corner at the joint.</li></ul>\n<p>Those points behave as free points until you constrain them.</p>\n<details><summary>It is a first-class element</summary>\n<p>It can have points on it, intersections and tangencies with lines, circles and arcs.</p>\n<p>The solver resolves them exactly, not approximately, together with the rest of the drawing.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Deformation</summary>\n<p>Dragging a point of the spline, or a point attached to it, deforms the curve and the rest of the drawing follows.</p>\n<p><b>Follower.</b> A follower (a point on the spline and on a straight line, for example) runs along the profile while you move the line.</p>\n<ul><li>A point attached to an <b>open</b> spline cannot go beyond its ends: if you try, the app warns of a conflict and undoes the move.</li><li>When you release a drag, a spline with relations recalculates its internal parameters so that it does not ripple if the points moved a lot; its shape may change slightly.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Spline button</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"spline\" title=\"Spline\"></span> <b>Spline button</b> (toolbar): tap to draw a spline.</p>\n<p><b>Dropdown.</b> Tap again or press and hold to open the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">dropdown</a>, which contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Trace point</li><li>Clear trace</li><li>Trace to spline</li><li>Close or open</li><li>Offset</li></ul>\n<p><b>What it asks for.</b> Trace to spline and Offset ask you for the number of points or the distance when you use them.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Trace point</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"trace\" title=\"Trace point\"></span> <b>Trace point</b> makes a point draw its path as the mechanism moves.</p>\n<p><b>Trace to spline.</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"spline\" title=\"Trace to spline\"></span> <b>Trace to spline</b> converts the path (the first turn) into an editable spline.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Offset</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"spline\" title=\"Offset\"></span> <b>Offset</b> is a curve parallel to a spline at a given distance (for example the roller radius of a cam).</p>\n<p>It warns you if the distance exceeds the radius of curvature.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Close / open</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"spline-closed\" title=\"Close or open the spline\"></span> <b>Close / open</b> the selected spline.</p>\n<p>In the cut SVG and in the DXF they come out as exact curves.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Fixed shape of a spline</summary>\n<p><b>Fixed shape of a spline</b> (spline properties) ties its control points to each other with hidden distances.</p>\n<p><b>Effect.</b> The curve moves and turns like a rigid body and <b>does not deform</b> when something drags one of its points (for example, the profile of a gear tooth that turns with its wheel).</p>\n<p><b>Clearing it.</b> Clear it to edit the curve point by point again.</p>\n<p><b>With arrays.</b> Use it before copying the spline in an array.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "simulaciones": "<details data-ch=\"simulaciones\"><summary>Graphical simulations</summary>\n<p>Moving the drawing over time: rotary and linear actuators, the simulation player, graphs of measurements, gears, rack and pinion, and relations that only hold while the elements touch.</p>\n<details><summary>Actuators</summary>\n<p>The <b>Actuators</b> (group after <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"fillet-f-and-chamfer\">Fillet</a>) move the drawing according to time <code>t</code>: <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"act-r\" title=\"Rotary actuator\"></span> <b>MR</b> (rotary) and <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"act-l\" title=\"Linear actuator\"></span> <b>ML</b> (linear). The <b>Simulation</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"play\" title=\"Simulation\"></span> plays them.</p>\n<h4>MR (rotary)</h4>\n<p>It is defined by choosing the line and the end of it that will be the center of rotation.</p>\n<ul><li>Tap them in any order, or select them before choosing the tool.</li><li>It turns counterclockwise by default, or clockwise; the direction is shown by the circular arrow.</li></ul>\n<h4>ML (linear)</h4>\n<p>It is defined with <b>a line and one of its ends</b>: tap the line and the end that will be the <b>head</b> (in any order), or select them before choosing the tool. The tail is the other end, and the actuator name stays at the midpoint of the line (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"head-and-tail-one-gesture\">Head and tail</a>).</p>\n<p>You can also define it with <b>two points</b>: tap (or select) first the <b>tail</b> point and then the <b>head</b> point. A tap on an empty place creates the point.</p>\n<h4>Value of the motion</h4>\n<p>Each actuator is given a <b>displacement</b> or a <b>speed</b> with respect to time <code>t</code>, in one of these ways:</p>\n<ul><li>An expression: <code>90*t</code>, <code>30*sin(2π*t)</code>.</li><li>A \"time; value\" table.</li></ul>\n<h4>Edit and delete</h4>\n<p>Tap their label, or their name in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"element-tree\">element tree</a> (group \"Actuators\"), to edit or delete them. The <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"filter\">selection filter</a> can turn off \"Actuators and graphs\".</p>\n<h4>Simulation</h4>\n<p><b>Simulation</b> opens the time bar: it plays and moves the drawing by solving the constraints. The MR line cannot be fixed horizontal or vertical, nor its length changed in the ML.</p>\n<p>The player has:</p>\n<ul><li>Step back and step forward.</li><li>Loop repeat (↻).</li><li>Back-and-forth (⇄).</li><li>Speed from 0.5x to 2.0x.</li></ul>\n<p>When you stop, the drawing returns to how it was.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Graphs</summary>\n<p>The <b>Graphs</b> tool <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"graph\" title=\"Graph\"></span> (actuator group) draws relations between variables. Tap where the lower left corner goes and type the relations.</p>\n<h4>Relations</h4>\n<p>They are written \"variable1 vs variable2\", one per line. The first goes on the vertical axis.</p>\n<ul><li>With actuators and time: <code>MR1 vs t</code>, <code>MR1.v vs t</code>, <code>D1 vs t</code>.</li><li>With two vectors of the same size, by name or with an expression (with <code>x:0..1;10</code> and <code>f(x):x.^2</code>): <code>f(x) vs x</code>, <code>x.^2 vs x</code>, <code>sin(x) vs x</code>.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Variables.</b> You can use:</p>\n<ul><li><code>t</code>.</li><li>The actuators (<code>MR1</code>, <code>ML1</code>); their speed with <code>.v</code>, for example <code>ML1.v</code>.</li><li>The named dimensions.</li><li>The variables of the texts.</li><li>The vectors.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Operations.</b> Each side accepts operations and functions with those variables: <code>p1.x/p1.y vs t</code>, <code>ML1.d-p1.x vs t</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Reading order.</b> The graph is read at its place on the page, in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\" data-topic=\"reading-order\">reading order</a> (from top to bottom and, within a row, from left to right, by its upper left corner), like a dimension with a formula. It sees the variables and functions defined <b>before</b> it; what is defined or redefined further down does not affect it.</p>\n<p><b>Time.</b> The time <code>t</code> is defined only by the simulation player (its \"to\"): the graph no longer has an end time.</p>\n<h4>The x variable</h4>\n<p>The <b>x</b> belongs to each graph.</p>\n<ul><li>If the drawing already defines <code>x</code> before the graph (a range or a vector), those values are used one by one.</li><li>If not, the graph uses a generic vector of <i>Samples</i> values between the limits of its <b>x1</b> axis (from −10 to 10 if you do not set them), so <code>sin(x)/x vs x</code> is drawn without defining anything.</li></ul>\n<p>What you see is what gets sampled: with <code>x'cm</code> and the axis from 0 to 900 mm, x goes from 0 to 90 cm.</p>\n<h4>Axes and window</h4>\n<p><b>With no limits on x1 and an x with units</b>, the graph <b>focuses itself</b>: the window goes from 0 to the largest of the measurements with that magnitude that the relation reads, also inside its functions (with a beam of L = 900 mm, from 0 to 900 mm).</p>\n<p><b>If there are no such measurements</b> (an x without units, or cuts written as numbers, <code>fsn(x, 0.5, 0)</code>), the window is the stretch where the curve does something (jumps, kinks or curvature): outside it, the curve is constant or straight.</p>\n<p><b>A curve that does something over the whole range</b> (<code>sin(x)/x</code>, <code>x^2</code>) stays from −10 to 10.</p>\n<p><b>With no limits on y</b>, the y window is that of what is seen in the x window.</p>\n<h4>Graph controls</h4>\n<p>With the graph selected (a tap, or when dragging it), controls appear on its sides; unselected, it looks clean, only the graph. There are four elastic sliders:</p>\n<ul><li>Top: horizontal pan (⇆).</li><li>Right: vertical pan (⇅).</li><li>Bottom: horizontal zoom (↔).</li><li>Left: vertical zoom (↕).</li></ul>\n<p>While you hold them away from the center the window moves (to the right or upward, or the opposite) or zooms in (right or up) or out, faster the further you move them. The pan is slow near the center, for fine adjustment. When you release them they return to the center.</p>\n<p>The <b>⤢ Fit</b> button in the upper right corner puts all the axes in automatic limits with one tap (the saved view does not change).</p>\n<p>Moving or zooming the window changes the axis limits (also in the graph's properties) and a generic x is recalculated within them.</p>\n<h4>Saved view</h4>\n<p>A double tap on a slider returns that axis to the <b>saved view</b>.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Saving it:</b> leave the graph at the position and zoom you want and tap the round button in the corner, between the zoom sliders (it fills in and saves the limits of x1 and y1). Afterwards you can move and zoom freely and the double tap recovers it.</li><li><b>Deleting it:</b> a double tap on that button deletes the saved view.</li><li><b>Without a saved view,</b> the double tap on a slider returns the axis to automatic limits.</li></ul>\n<h4>Secondary axes</h4>\n<p>At the end of the line, <code>@y2</code>, <code>@x2</code> or <code>@x2 y2</code> draw the relation on the secondary axes: x1 bottom, x2 top, y1 left, y2 right.</p>\n<h4>Style of each relation</h4>\n<p>It goes in quotes at the end of the relation, and combines:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Color:</b> <code>r g b c m y k w</code> or <code>#rrggbb</code>; <code>k</code> is the text color.</li><li><b>Line:</b> <code>-</code> solid, <code>--</code> dashed, <code>:</code> dotted, <code>-.</code> dash-dot.</li><li><b>Symbol:</b> <code>o + * . x s d ^ v &gt; &lt; p h</code> (circle, plus, asterisk, point, cross, square, diamond, triangles, star and hexagon).</li></ul>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul><li><code>σ vs N \"rx\"</code>: a red cross with no line.</li><li><code>y vs x \"k--d\"</code>: black dashed with diamonds.</li><li><code>Y vs X \"o\"</code>: only circles, a <b>scatter plot</b>, to see how data are distributed (for example, from <code>randn</code>).</li></ul>\n<p>With a symbol and no line, the line is not drawn. Without quotes, a solid line in the color that comes next in order. A relation of a single point without a symbol is marked with a circle.</p>\n<p>In the graph's properties, below the relations, each one has its lists of color, line and symbol, which write that style.</p>\n<h4>Properties</h4>\n<p>Tap a graph to select it (it is highlighted): drag it to move it and drag its edges or corners to resize it.</p>\n<p>A double tap opens its <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"properties\">properties</a>:</p>\n<ul><li>Title.</li><li>Relations.</li><li>Samples.</li><li>Position.</li><li>Size.</li><li>For each axis: its free name (for example <code>p12.x [cm]</code>, which serves as documentation because the axes do not show units), linear or logarithmic scale, minimum and maximum, and major and minor divisions.</li></ul>\n<h4>Points with a trajectory</h4>\n<p>Points with <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"splines\" data-topic=\"trace-point\">\"Trace point\"</a> draw their trajectory when the simulation runs and keep it when you stop it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Gear</summary>\n<p><b>Gear</b> (Constraints group) links the rotation of two gears with a rotation ratio.</p>\n<h4>Creating the relation</h4>\n<ol><li>First position the gears by hand, with the teeth not interfering.</li><li>Select 2 lines, one rigid with each gear (for example the radius of each one that starts at the fixed center).</li><li>Type the rotation ratio and create the relation.</li></ol>\n<p>The pose at that moment is the starting point and from then on the rotation of both is governed by the ratio.</p>\n<h4>The rotation ratio</h4>\n<ul><li>It is negative if they turn in opposite directions.</li><li>It accepts a formula, e.g. <code>-17/34</code>.</li><li>With equal modules it is −z1/z2, or the ratio of the pitch or base diameters, not of the outside ones.</li></ul>\n<h4>Use and conditions</h4>\n<p>Drag any point of one of the gears and the other turns with the ratio, for several turns.</p>\n<p>Conditions: each gear turns around a fixed point (the end of the line that coincides with it) and its points are tied to the center, like those of a <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dividir\" data-topic=\"circular-array\">circular array</a>.</p>\n<p>It does not model tooth contact. If you change <code>z</code> or <code>m</code>, position again and create the relation again.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Rack</summary>\n<p><b>Rack</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"rack\" title=\"Rack\"></span> (wheel and rack, Constraints group) links the rotation of a wheel with the advance of a point.</p>\n<h4>Creating the relation</h4>\n<p>Select, in this order:</p>\n<ol><li>The radial line of the wheel.</li><li>The line of the rack (or the ground).</li><li>The point that advances.</li></ol>\n<p>Then type the ratio.</p>\n<h4>The ratio</h4>\n<p>It is how far the point advances for each radian the wheel turns (signed; the radius of the wheel). A wheel rolling to the right on the ground turns clockwise, so the ratio is −radius.</p>\n<h4>Which point to choose</h4>\n<ul><li>For a wheel rolling without slipping, the point is its <b>center</b> (not a point on the rim: the app checks this and, if you chose the end of the wheel line at the rim, it uses the other end).</li><li>For a pinion with its rack, a point of the rack.</li></ul>\n<h4>Use and conditions</h4>\n<p>Drag the wheel or the point and the other moves with the ratio.</p>\n<ul><li>The pinion must turn around a fixed point (or one tied to a fixed point with <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"coincident\">\"Coincident\"</a>).</li><li>The rack line gives the direction of advance.</li><li>It does not model tooth contact.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Finite relations</summary>\n<p><b>Finite relations</b> (\"Only within limits\", in the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"properties\">properties</a> of an element, in the list of its constraints) make a relation hold only while the elements touch.</p>\n<h4>By default and with the box checked</h4>\n<ul><li><b>By default,</b> a tangency, a point on a line or on an arc holds even if the elements are not seen in contact (for the solver, lines and circles are infinite).</li><li><b>With the box checked,</b> the relation only holds while the elements <b>touch graphically</b>: their contact point falls within the limits (the segment between its ends, the sweep of the arc) and the separation is almost zero. Outside that, the elements are left free.</li></ul>\n<p>If they cross each other within a step, they are captured.</p>\n<h4>Stop at edges</h4>\n<p>With <b>Stop at edges</b> (it appears when you check \"Only within limits\") the element is not left free when it goes out through an edge: it stops there and the relation resumes when it goes back inside.</p>\n<ul><li>The point stays at the end of the segment or of the arc.</li><li>In a tangency, the end of the segment or of the arc rests on the other element.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example: Geneva drive</h4>\n<p>It is useful for slots and contacts that begin and end. A Geneva drive is built with a finite \"point on line\" relation between the pin and each slot (the drive advances one slot per turn of the driver).</p>\n<p>With ideal geometry (a/d = sin(π/n)) the pin enters head-on through the mouth; with any other, it enters from the side and the drive makes a jump when capturing it.</p>\n<h4>Where it is available</h4>\n<ul><li>Point on line.</li><li>Point on circle or arc.</li><li>Line-circle or arc tangent.</li><li>Tangent between arcs.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Libraries</summary>\n<p>A <b>Library</b> is a document with functions, scripts and tables that you want to use in others.</p>\n<h4>Creating a library</h4>\n<p>In <b>Settings → Library</b> check \"It is a library\" and fill in its card:</p>\n<ul><li>Name.</li><li><b>Alias</b> (a short prefix, e.g. <code>UF</code>).</li><li>Author.</li><li>Description.</li><li>What is <b>public</b>: by default, its functions and scripts. What those need travels too, as private, and the examples do not travel.</li></ul>\n<h4>Saving</h4>\n<p>\"Save to <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"github\">GitHub</a>\" leaves it in the <code>MyLibrary/</code> folder of the repository, with the extension <code>.alib.json</code> and a version that goes up by itself (v1, v2…). If it had been opened from another folder, it offers to send the original to the trash.</p>\n<p>\"Open from GitHub\" has the <b>Libraries</b> tab to reopen it.</p>\n<h4>Using it in another document</h4>\n<ol><li>Choose <b><a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"file-button\">File</a> → Insert library…</b>.</li><li>Choose it and confirm the alias.</li></ol>\n<p>A block <code>▸ UtilidadesFatiga (UF) · v3</code> remains with a copy of its definitions (it works offline).</p>\n<p>Its names are used with the alias: <code>UF.C_(surf)(520'MPa, \"machined\")=</code>, <code>UF.Neuber_(steels)_(1,1)=</code>. The prefix is drawn in gray.</p>\n<p>It applies from its place on the page, like any definition, and its functions always use their own tables even if your document has others with the same name.</p>\n<h4>Updating</h4>\n<p>If the library changes on GitHub, the block shows <code>⟳ v4</code>. In its properties (double tap), \"Update\" brings in the new version and first tells you which names are added or disappear.</p>\n<h4>Decimal separator and angle mode</h4>\n<ul><li>If the library uses another decimal separator, it is converted when inserted.</li><li>If it uses another angle mode, the block warns you.</li></ul>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "estilo": "<details data-ch=\"estilo\"><summary>Style, layers and view</summary>\n<p>Color, line type, width and fills; layers; what the view shows; and the element tree.</p>\n<details><summary>Style</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"palette\" title=\"Style\"></span> <b>Style</b> sets the color, the line type and the width. It applies to the selection and to new elements.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Color.</b></li><li><b>Line type:</b> continuous, hidden, axis, phantom, dashed, dotted, trace and point, or a new one with its own pattern.</li><li><b>Width:</b> thin, normal, thick.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Fill / hatch</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"hatch\" title=\"Fill / hatch\"></span> <b>Fill / hatch</b> is applied on a closed outline (lines, arcs or a circle).</p>\n<p>It can be lines, cross-hatch or solid, with angle, spacing and opacity.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Layers</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"layers\" title=\"Layers\"></span> <b>Layers</b> (top left corner of the drawing) opens the list of layers.</p>\n<p>At first there are none, and what you draw belongs to no layer.</p>\n<h4>Create, choose and delete</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Create layer:</b> makes the new layer the <b>current layer</b> (checked circle). Whatever you draw afterwards, including points, texts and fills, goes on it.</li><li><b>None:</b> the \"None\" row goes back to drawing with no layer.</li><li><b>Delete:</b> the trash can <b>deletes</b> the layer, and its elements become \"none\".</li><li><b>Rename:</b> you can rename a layer in its row.</li><li><b>Name on screen:</b> when there is a <b>current layer</b>, its name appears under the Layers button.</li></ul>\n<h4>Turn off and on</h4>\n<p>The eye <b>turns the layer off or on</b>.</p>\n<p>When it is off, its elements, the points used only by them, and their constraints and dimensions are not visible, cannot be selected, do not cut when <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"seleccionar\" data-topic=\"trim-x\">trimming or extending</a>, and are not exported. They still exist and the solver respects them.</p>\n<p>Turning off the current layer leaves you with no current layer.</p>\n<h4>Change the layer of an element</h4>\n<p>To change the layer of an element, double-tap it.</p>\n<p>Copy and paste, split and trim keep the layer of the original.</p>\n<h4>Dimensions and leaders</h4>\n<p><b>Dimensions</b> and <b>leaders</b> also have a layer: the current one when you create them, and you change it in their window, with a double tap.</p>\n<p>With the layer off they are not visible and are not exported, but a dimension still measures or drives through its formula, for example one that moves a part from a calculation.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>View</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"eye\" title=\"View\"></span> <b>View</b> gathers these options:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Grid.</b></li><li><b>Magnet</b> (to the grid).</li><li><b>Soft lock</b> (and every how many degrees).</li><li><b>Time for the snap</b> to intersections.</li><li><b>Show or hide</b> constraints, points, dimensions and texts.</li><li><b>Finger draws.</b></li><li><b>Panel labels.</b></li><li><b>Dash scale.</b></li></ul>\n<p><b>Version and build.</b> In the bottom left corner of the drawing you see in italics the <b>app version and its build</b>, the commit it was compiled from (for example <i>v1.6.1 7bc0108</i>; with \"+\" if it had uncommitted changes), so you know exactly which build each device is running.</p>\n<p><b>Current file.</b> In the top right corner you see the <b>name of the current file</b> (or \"unnamed\").</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Element tree</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"list-tree\" title=\"Element tree\"></span> <b>Element tree</b> (first button of the bar) lists lines, circles, arcs, splines, texts, fills, points and relations.</p>\n<p>Tap an element to select it and see its points and constraints.</p>\n<h4>One place for each thing</h4>\n<p>Each thing has a single place of its own.</p>\n<p><b>Points.</b> A point lives in the first element that uses it (centers, in their circle) and appears in the others as a link <i>↗</i> (\"↗ Point p1 (in Circle c2)\"). Under \"Loose points\" you only find those that belong to no element.</p>\n<p><b>Links.</b> Tapping a link selects the element and opens its own place.</p>\n<h4>Relations and dimensions</h4>\n<p>Relations live in \"Relations and dimensions\", separated into Dimensions, Geometric, Mechanical and Automatic.</p>\n<p><b>Automatic.</b> They are grouped by the operation that created them, numbered in the order it was done (\"Circular array 2\", \"Fillet 1\", \"Mirror copy 1\", \"Chamfer\", \"Split\", \"Polygon\"), and those of fixed shapes and vectors by their element.</p>\n<p><b>No origin.</b> Those with no origin (drawings made before this version, or those the magnet adds when drawing on an element) are grouped by type.</p>\n<p><b>Per element.</b> Under each element there is a single \"Relations (n)\" row with links to its own and to those of its points, which also counts the automatic ones.</p>\n<h4>Group by layer</h4>\n<p>If the drawing has layers, the layers button in the header groups the tree <b>by layer</b>.</p>\n<p>First comes each layer (empty ones too), with its eye to turn it on or off, and inside it the same groups by type. Then \"No layer\" and the relations, actuators, graphs and paths, which have no layer.</p>\n<p>A point follows its element even if it is on another layer.</p>\n<h4>Outlines</h4>\n<p>The <b>Outlines</b> group joins the lines and arcs with stroke (not construction), elliptical arcs and open splines chained by their ends: \"Outline 2 · closed · 4 lines, 2 arcs\" (closed, open or branched if three or more meet at a node).</p>\n<p><b>When two ends touch.</b> Two ends touch if they are the same point, if a <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\" data-topic=\"coincident\">coincidence</a> ties them, or if they are in the same place with nothing tying them. Those are flagged (\"1 join without constraint\"), because they can open when the drawing is moved; to tie them, add a coincidence.</p>\n<p><b>Selecting.</b> Tapping an outline selects all its elements; inside it they appear as links, in the order they are traversed.</p>\n<p><b>Gap.</b> If a free end is near another (within 1 % of the drawing size), the outline says how large that <b>gap</b> is and with which outline (\"gap 0.0143 with 2\"), or \"between its ends\" if it almost closes by itself. This is the typical reason a profile that should be closed comes out open.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "archivo": "<details data-ch=\"archivo\"><summary>File and export</summary>\n<p>Saving and opening drawings (also in a GitHub repository) and exporting to PNG, SVG, DXF and SVG for laser cutting.</p>\n<details><summary>File button</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"save\" title=\"File\"></span> <b>File button</b> (bar, between the menu and help) opens the <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\" data-topic=\"dropdown-buttons\">dropdown</a> with:</p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"file-plus\" title=\"New drawing\"></span> <b>New</b></li><li><b>Save</b></li><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"folder-open\" title=\"Open\"></span> <b>Open</b></li><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"image\" title=\"PNG\"></span> <b>PNG</b></li><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"svg\" title=\"SVG\"></span> <b>SVG</b></li><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"svg\" title=\"SVG for laser cutting\"></span> <b>SVG for laser cutting</b></li><li><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dxf\" title=\"DXF\"></span> <b>DXF</b></li></ul>\n<p>They used to be in a section of the tools menu.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>GitHub</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"folder-open\" title=\"Open from GitHub\"></span> <b>GitHub</b> (File → Save to GitHub / Open from GitHub / <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"props\" title=\"Set up GitHub\"></span> Set up GitHub) saves and opens .json files in a <b>private repository</b>.</p>\n<p>It lets you continue a drawing on another device and keep the version history (each save is a commit).</p>\n<h4>Setup</h4>\n<p>Done once per device. You are asked for:</p>\n<ul><li>Repository (<code>user/repository</code>).</li><li>Branch.</li><li>Folder.</li><li>A <b>fine-grained personal access token</b> (GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Fine-grained tokens) limited to that single repository with the \"Contents: Read and write\" permission.</li></ul>\n<h4>Token security</h4>\n<p>The token stays only in this browser's storage, does not go into the files or the .json.</p>\n<p>Anyone with access to your browser could use it, which is why the minimum permission and a repository used only for drawings are advisable.</p>\n<h4>Conflicts and connection</h4>\n<p>If the file changed on GitHub since the last time you opened or saved it here (for example, you edited it on another device), the app asks before overwriting. There is no automatic merge: one version wins.</p>\n<p>It does not work offline (local saving is still available).</p>\n<h4>History</h4>\n<p>The history is visible on GitHub, in the file's tab → History.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Managing GitHub files</summary>\n<p><b>GitHub files</b> (File → <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"github\">Open from GitHub</a>) is the list of the drawings in the repository. Tapping a name opens it.</p>\n<h4>Select and act on several</h4>\n<p>Each row has a checkbox to <b>select several</b> (\"All\" / \"None\"). On the selection:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Trash:</b> moves to the repository's <code>_papelera</code> subfolder, does not delete.</li><li><b>Rename:</b> one at a time.</li><li><b>Duplicate:</b> creates \"name copia.json\".</li><li><b>Protect</b> / \"Remove protection\".</li></ul>\n<h4>Trash</h4>\n<p>The <b>Trash</b> tab lets you <b>Restore</b> or <b>Delete permanently</b>.</p>\n<p>Delete permanently is irreversible from the app; the file remains in GitHub's git history.</p>\n<h4>Protect</h4>\n<p>A <b>protected</b> file (🔒) asks for a second confirmation to go to the trash and another to be deleted completely.</p>\n<p>The mark is saved in the repository (<code>_ipad-cad.json</code>, inside the configured folder), so it applies on all your devices, and travels with the file when it is renamed or moved.</p>\n<h4>Move, rename and batches</h4>\n<p>Moving and renaming create the destination and then delete the original: if something fails halfway you are left with an extra copy, the file is never lost.</p>\n<p>Each operation is a commit. In large batches GitHub may throttle consecutive writes, and the notice lists what could not be done.</p>\n<h4>Current drawing</h4>\n<p>Above the list appears <b>Current drawing</b>: the automatic save of this device (the time of the last save and how many elements it has), with \"Save to GitHub\" and \"Discard and start new\".</p>\n<p>It exists only on this device and reopens by itself when the app starts.</p>\n<h4>Date, search and sort</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Date:</b> each file shows the <b>date of its last change</b> (requested from GitHub in the background, one per file; a renamed or restored file counts from that move).</li><li><b>Search box:</b> filters by name, ignoring case and accents. What the filter hides is not selected and does not receive actions.</li><li><b>Sort:</b> the button toggles name / date (most recent first).</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Save / Open</summary>\n<p>The drawing is saved to a .json file (it asks for the name).</p>\n<p>In addition, the app remembers the last drawing <b>only in this browser</b>: save a .json to back it up.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Device data</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"folder-open\" title=\"Data\"></span> <b>File → Data…</b> shows the data folder of this device, shared by all drawings: the texts saved by <code>wfile(valor, Nombre)</code> and read by <code>rfile(Nombre)</code> (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\" data-topic=\"data-between-drawings\">Data between drawings</a>), and the audio files.</p>\n<h4>Import and manage</h4>\n<p><b>Import…</b> adds a file. An audio file is asked for with a name, is reduced to one channel, 16 kHz and 8 bits, about 16 KB per second, and plays with <code>play(\"nombre\")</code> (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\" data-topic=\"sounds\">Sounds</a>).</p>\n<p>Each item can be <b>tested</b> (audio), <b>exported</b> and <b>deleted</b>.</p>\n<h4>What travels with the drawing</h4>\n<ul><li>When a drawing is saved it carries the audio files it names in quotes (also inside a library).</li><li>When opened, it restores them if they are missing on the device, without overwriting one that already exists with that name (if it is different, it warns and uses the one on the device).</li><li>A name built from a variable, such as <code>play(n)</code>, is not detected and does not travel with the drawing.</li><li>The <code>wfile</code> texts do not travel with the drawing.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>PNG</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"image\" title=\"PNG\"></span> <b>PNG</b> is an image of the drawing.</p>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"svg\" title=\"SVG\"></span> <b>SVG</b>: vector.</p>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dxf\" title=\"DXF\"></span> <b>DXF</b>: for CAD (dimensions come out as geometry and text).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>SVG CUT</summary>\n<p><span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"svg\" title=\"SVG for laser cutting\"></span> <b>SVG CUT</b> (laser cutting): SVG at real scale in mm.</p>\n<p>Each <i>line class</i> (type, color and width) is sent to:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Cut</b> (cut color, red by default).</li><li><b>Mark</b> (another color, for example folds).</li><li><b>Skip.</b></li></ul>\n<p>It comes out in two groups, \"cut\" and \"mark\". Dimensions, texts, points and fills are not exported.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "atajos": "<details data-ch=\"atajos\"><summary>Keyboard shortcuts</summary>\n<p>The keys for each tool, for working with the keyboard.</p>\n<details><summary>Tools</summary>\n<p>One letter, without Ctrl.</p>\n<ul><li><b>V</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"select\" title=\"Select\"></span> select, <b>P</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"point\" title=\"Point\"></span> point, <b>I</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"intersect\" title=\"Intersection\"></span> intersection.</li><li><b>L</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"line\" title=\"Line\"></span> line, <b>K</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"cline\" title=\"Construction line\"></span> construction line, <b>M</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"mline\" title=\"Midpoint line\"></span> midpoint line, <b>W</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"vector\" title=\"Vector\"></span> vector.</li><li><b>C</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"circle\" title=\"Circle\"></span> circle, <b>O</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"ellipse\" title=\"Ellipse\"></span> ellipse, <b>R</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"arc-c\" title=\"Arc by center\"></span> arc by center, <b>A</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"arc-3\" title=\"Arc by 3 points\"></span> arc by 3 points, <b>S</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"spline\" title=\"Spline\"></span> spline.</li><li><b>B</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"rect\" title=\"Rectangle\"></span> rectangle, <b>G</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"polygon\" title=\"Polygon\"></span> polygon.</li><li><b>T</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"text\" title=\"Text\"></span> text, <b>D</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"dim-dist\" title=\"Dimension\"></span> dimension.</li><li><b>X</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"trim\" title=\"Trim\"></span> trim, <b>E</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"extend\" title=\"Extend\"></span> extend, <b>F</b> <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"fillet\" title=\"Fillet\"></span> fillet.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Editing</summary>\n<ul><li><b>Ctrl/Cmd+C</b> copy and <b>Ctrl/Cmd+V</b> paste.</li><li><b>Ctrl/Cmd+Z</b> undo, <b>Ctrl/Cmd+Y</b> (or <b>Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Z</b>) redo.</li><li><b>Del</b> or <b>Backspace</b> deletes the selection.</li><li><b>Esc</b> cancels or finishes what you are doing.</li></ul>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "limites": "<details data-ch=\"limites\"><summary>Known limits</summary>\n<p>What the app does not do yet or does only under certain conditions. Worth reading before a big job.</p>\n<details><summary>Ellipses and splines</summary>\n<ul><li>The <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"ellipse\" title=\"Ellipse\"></span> ellipse does not support intersections or tangency with splines. Where an ellipse crosses a spline or another ellipse, trimming or extending is computed on the drawn curve (a very close approximation, not exact).</li><li>A tangency with a spline may fail to converge if the contact point starts very far from the solution (the app starts it at the closest point).</li></ul>\n<h4>Trimmed spline</h4>\n<p>A trimmed spline keeps its number of points and spaces them at equal distances over what remains (if the cut splits it in two, they are spaced according to the length of each piece). It looks very much like the original, but it is not identical.</p>\n<ul><li>If the spline has few points and with them it would drift from the curve (by more than 0.1% of its size), the necessary ones are added.</li><li>If it has relationships or any of its points is fixed or used by another element, the points do not move and only a few are added next to the cut.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Solver and constraints</summary>\n<ul><li>Copies in a circular array and symmetries do not load the solver: a point that is only a copy of another is computed from its source after solving (the solver has fixed memory and ran out at about 220 copied points). If you add another constraint to a copy point, or use it in a curve with relationships (point on spline, tangency), it becomes an unknown again and counts against that limit.</li><li>If the solver does not converge or there are conflicting constraints, the action is undone with a warning.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Drawing, dimensioning and copies</summary>\n<ul><li>The midpoint as a drawing snap is only offered on lines (not on arcs, ellipses or splines) and not on lines that are very short on screen.</li><li>Dimensioning: a spline cannot be dimensioned against an arc or circle (it has no center), and the distance between two parallel lines carries no sign.</li><li>Splitting arcs and circles into equal parts does not leave an equal-angle constraint.</li><li>The angular dimensions of a circular array cannot be edited by hand (the array is redone); the distance of a linear array can.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Layers</summary>\n<p>The <span class=\"hi\" data-icon=\"layers\" title=\"Layers\"></span> <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"estilo\" data-topic=\"layers\">layers</a> only group elements (visibility and current layer); they have no color or line type of their own.</p>\n<p>A point that is part of an element follows that element's layer. Changing the layer of a whole selection has to be done element by element, from its properties window.</p>\n<p>A constraint or dimension that joins a visible element with a hidden one is also hidden while the layer is off.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Graphs with simulation in Chrome on iPad</summary>\n<p>Computing a graph that simulates the mechanism (for example <code>p41.y vs p41.x</code> with actuators) can close the page in Chrome on iPad; in Safari, on PC and on the phone it works.</p>\n<p><b>What was observed.</b> With the test drawing <code>prueba-simulaciones.json</code>: if the page closed while computing, on reopening the graphs are left \"paused\" with a notice of the progress reached, and computing again (one tap on the graph) can fail again, each time, with the drawing saved automatically. The same file opened from <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"github\">GitHub</a> (<a href=\"#\" data-help=\"archivo\" data-topic=\"save--open\">Open</a>) loads without problems.</p>\n<p><b>What does not fail.</b> Moving or resizing the graph, and panning or zooming, do not fail (they do not recompute).</p>\n<p><b>Cause.</b> It is not yet confirmed.</p>\n<p><b>In the meantime.</b> Open the file from GitHub instead of reopening the automatic save, or use Safari.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "acerca": "<details data-ch=\"acerca\"><summary>About</summary>\n<p>The solver that resolves the constraints, the libraries the app uses, and where the version history is.</p>\n<details><summary>Solver and libraries</summary>\n<p><b>Solver.</b> The constraints are solved by <b>PlaneGCS</b>, the 2D geometric solver of the <i>Sketcher</i> module of <b>FreeCAD</b>, whose original code is by <b>Konstantinos Poulios</b> (© 2011, part of the FreeCAD project, LGPL license).</p>\n<p><b>Package.</b> The app uses it compiled to WebAssembly through the <b>@salusoft89/planegcs</b> package (v1.3.0), a wasm build and JavaScript wrapper by <b>Salusoft89</b>, licensed LGPL-2.0-or-later.</p>\n<p><b>Links.</b></p>\n<ul><li>PlaneGCS: <a href=\"https://github.com/Salusoft89/planegcs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">github.com/Salusoft89/planegcs</a></li><li>FreeCAD: <a href=\"https://www.freecad.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">freecad.org</a></li></ul>\n<p><b>Other components.</b> <b>Lucide</b> icons (ISC license) and bundling with <b>Vite</b> (MIT license).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Splines in PlaneGCS</summary>\n<p>Splines are <b>native cubic B-splines of PlaneGCS</b>: each spline point lies on its knot, the poles are auxiliary unknowns of the solver, and the drawn curve is the same one the solver solves.</p>\n<p><b>Exact relationships.</b> The point on spline (<code>point_on_bspline</code>) and the tangencies with lines, circles and arcs (<code>angle_via_point_and_param</code>) are solved exactly.</p>\n<p><b>Open splines.</b> In open ones, zero curvature at the ends is expressed with a midpoint constraint between poles.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Version history</summary>\n<p>The detail of each version is in <a href=\"https://github.com/oscampo/a-cad/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CHANGELOG.md</a>, and the full manual in <a href=\"https://github.com/oscampo/a-cad/tree/main/docs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">docs/</a>.</p>\n</details>\n</details>"
 }
};
